window.__INITIAL_DATA__ = {"projects":[{"id":"project-1","folderName":"Project_1_Student_Resource_Hub","name":"Project 1 Student Resource Hub","faq":"# Student Resource & Academic Support Hub — FAQ\n\n**Q: Why should I trust this is actually free? Where is the budget coming from?** A: The platform is built using free, open-source technology and run locally. Council funds are strictly reserved for actual student research grants, not for hosting fees or expensive software licenses. We do not monetize your data or charge access fees.\n\n**Q: What if the Shared Flashcard Library has wrong answers and causes me to fail an exam?** A: The flashcards are peer-reviewed study tools. Every card features a 'Report Error' button. Any card flagged three times is automatically hidden until reviewed and corrected by the Academics Committee. However, these are supplementary aids and should not replace your official course materials.\n\n**Q: What happens if a professor refuses to follow the Syllabus Transparency Portal format?** A: The standardized syllabus format is backed by a written agreement with the College Dean's Office. If a professor refuses to comply, students can use the Centralized Grievance Channel to report the non-compliance. The council will then mediate the issue directly with the administration on the student's behalf.\n\n**Q: Isn't this just a glorified link-tree that duplicates things the university already offers?** A: The university does not offer a unified academic pathing tool, a localized research job board, or an integrated scholarship calculator that accurately accounts for specific student ID formats. This platform centralizes fragmented resources and builds the critical tools the administration has left empty.\n\n**Q: How do we know the contact numbers and procedures in the COS Student Survival Guide are actually updated and reliable?** A: All information regarding shifting, enlistment, and grievances is cross-verified directly with the Dean’s Office and respective departments at the beginning of every term by the Director for Academics to ensure nothing is outdated.\n\n**Q: What is HomeCOStasis, and why is it being continued from the past administration?** A: HomeCOStasis was initiated under the AY 2025–2026 administration (Clark Cuaresma, ARC: G-SCG-25260011) as a comprehensive student navigation kit across Google Drive, Google Sheets, and social media. Rather than abandoning valuable student resources when administrations change, President Pauline S. Galias is continuing and revitalizing HomeCOStasis for AY 2026–2027. All historical guides, flowchart trackers, and survival docs are being thoroughly audited and updated specifically for incoming ID 126 frosh, ensuring zero loss of institutional knowledge.\n\n**Q: How does the COS Bulletin Board differ from existing SCG social media pages?** A: While social media algorithms frequently bury urgent academic deadlines and departmental announcements, the COS Bulletin Board operates as a direct, chronological, and noise-free Telegram broadcast channel alongside website alerts. Initiated under the AY 2025–2026 administration (ARC: G-SCG-25260016) and continued under President Pauline S. Galias, it guarantees that critical university notices, enlistment advisories, and emergency announcements reach students in real time without algorithm suppression.","description":"# Project Description\n\nOne website that holds everything a student needs to stay enrolled, funded, and on track. If it helps a student survive the semester, it lives here.","executionPlan":"## **Project 1: Student Resource & Academic Support Hub**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Quick classification note:** Every sub-feature here is Bucket B for ongoing operations. The initial platform launch is filed as \"Others\" under the DAAM Types of Activity, which processes as a General Pre-Activity (APS) with a 4-6 day lead time. Dir. Documentations files this before any public announcement goes out. After the APS clears, individual sub-feature rollouts each go through P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) separately.  \n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Sub-Feature | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| COS Student Information Hub | Dir. Academics | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| HomeCOStasis Student Guide (ID 126 Refresh) | Dir. Academics | President | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| COS Bulletin Board (Telegram Broadcast) | Dir. Documentations | Chief Communications | President | Dir. Academics |\n| Scholarship Grade Calculator | Dir. Academics | President | Chief of Staff, Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| Academic Pathing Tool | Dir. Academics | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Syllabus Transparency Portal | Dir. Academics | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Note Generator | Dir. Academics | President | Dir. Creatives | Dir. Documentations |\n| Free Software Directory | Dir. Academics | President |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| LaTeX Tutorials & Live Seminar | Dir. Academics | President | Math Circle, PhySoc | Dir. Documentations |\n| NMAT Online Repository | Dir. Academics | President |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| Research Grants & Funding Portal | Dir. R\\&D | President | Chief of Staff, Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| Research Job Board | Dir. R\\&D | President | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Documentations |\n| Research Readiness Survey | Dir. R\\&D | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| P\\&M for all sub-features | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| All clearance and submission tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff |  |  |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\nThis runs in two phases. Phase 1 is every academic survival tool. Phase 2 is the research tools.\n\n**Pre-Launch (6 Weeks Before Term Start)**\n\nThe real work happens here. Waiting until term starts to chase the Syllabus LOA or ADSO confirmation is too late.\n\n*   **T-6 weeks:** Pauline initiates the LOA request to the College Dean for the Syllabus Transparency Portal. This is the longest dependency and the only feature that cannot go live without a signature.\n*   **T-5 weeks:** Dir. Academics contacts ADSO directly to confirm current scholarship retention criteria. Written confirmation only. A verbal answer is not enough.\n*   **T-4 weeks:** Web platform build starts. Dir. Academics inputs initial data: flowcharts, scholarship rules, department contacts for the Info Hub.\n*   **T-2 weeks:** Dir. Documentations submits the General Pre-Activity (APS) for the platform launch. 4-6 day lead time. This needs to clear before any public announcement goes out — P&M for individual sub-features is filed alongside it but the APS is the gate.\n*   **T-2 weeks:** Dir. Creatives submits batched P&M clearances for all Phase 1 sub-features. Submitting early leaves room for revisions without cutting into the 24-hour minimum.\n*   **T-1 week:** Dir. Academics and Dir. R&D sign off on data accuracy. Platform is ready before Day 1 of term.\n\n**Phase 1 Launch (Week 1 of Term)**\n\n*   **Day 1-3:** HomeCOStasis (Google Drive/Sheets archive refresh for ID 126), COS Bulletin Board (Telegram Channel & Broadcast sync), COS Student Information Hub, Scholarship Calculator, Academic Pathing Tool, Note Generator, Free Software Directory, LaTeX Tutorials, and NMAT Repository go live. GOSM posting follows immediately after P&M clears.\n*   **Day 3-5:** Syllabus Transparency Portal goes live once the written LOA is signed. This feature does not launch without it.\n\n**Phase 2 Launch (Weeks 2-3 of Term)**\n\n*   **Week 2:** Dir. Creatives submits P&M for the Grants Portal and Job Board. Dir. EXT/INT is looped in to push the Job Board to any external research contacts.\n*   **Week 2-3:** Research Grants Portal and Research Job Board go live after clearances confirm. \n*   **Week 3:** Research Readiness Survey launches alongside the Job Board. Dir. R&D monitors responses and starts drafting a data summary for the admin.\n*   **Week 6 (Wednesday, November 11, 2026):** Live Seminar: *LaTeX Essentials: Scientific Typesetting Seminar (Math & Physics Edition)* held in campus lecture hall (Velasco / Andrew Hall). Room reservation submitted via Letters & Requests at T-2 weeks; P&M publicity cleared at T-1 week; session recording and starter templates archived to H2Zero.ai within 48 hours.\n\n**Ongoing Maintenance**\n\n*   **Monthly:** Dir. Academics re-verifies Calculator and Pathing Tool data against any ADSO or Registrar updates. The \"Last Verified\" timestamp on each tool updates to reflect this.\n*   **End of Term:** Dir. R&D compiles Research Readiness Survey results and submits a brief report to the administration, with a formal RESAP recommendation if the data supports one.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\n*   GOSM Defense Week (Jan 19-24): No new sub-feature launches. Phase 1 should already be live by this point.\n*   Midterms (Feb 16-21): All promotional pushes pause. Platform stays live and operational.\n*   ILW (Mar 2-7): Same rule. Promotion pauses, platform runs.\n*   Activity Ban (Mar 30 - Apr 11): Unaffected. This is a standing platform, not an activity.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\nIn order of urgency. The first two are the only real blockers. Everything else can run in parallel.\n\n1.  **Syllabus Portal LOA from the Dean's Office (critical path):** Dir. Academics prepares the letter, Pauline signs and submits. Target: signed at least 3 weeks before term start. If this is delayed, the portal launch slides accordingly.\n2.  **ADSO scholarship criteria (written confirmation):** Dir. Academics establishes a recurring contact at ADSO and requests written confirmation at the start of every term. The Calculator carries a public \"Last Verified\" date so students can see how current the data is.\n3.  **P&M clearance for each sub-feature:** Dir. Creatives batches these during pre-launch so all Phase 1 features are cleared before Day 1.\n4.  **Web platform and data verification:** All tools built and tested before term starts. Dir. Academics confirms academic data accuracy. Dir. Creatives confirms genesis-DESIGN compliance before anything goes public.\n5.  **Research Job Board vetting standards:** Dir. R&D and Dir. EXT/INT agree on a posting checklist before the board goes live. No listing is published without a review.\n6.  **LaTeX Seminar Venue & Technical Setup:** Dir. Academics coordinates room reservation with the College Dean's Office / Facilities at least 2 weeks prior to November 11. Offline starter templates and Overleaf project links pre-compiled before the workshop.","subprojects":"# Project 1: Student Resource & Academic Support Hub Sub-Projects\n\n## COS Student Information Hub\n\n*   **Description:** Everything you need to navigate college life in one place: Enlistment and pre-enlistment guides, shifting procedures, department policies, a full contact list for every department office and head, and an FAQ section built from the questions students actually ask. No more chasing down information across five different people and three Facebook groups.\n\n## HomeCOStasis: COS Comprehensive Student Guide (Continued from AY 2025–2026)\n\n*   **Description:** Originally initiated under the AY 2025–2026 SCG administration (Clark Cuaresma) under ARC code `G-SCG-25260011`, HomeCOStasis is formally continued, modernized, and maintained by President Pauline S. Galias for AY 2026–2027. Recognizing that scattered university processes cause immense confusion, this initiative serves as the master centralized information kit integrating Google Drive archives, enrollment sheets, visual explainers, and local offline access via H2Zero.ai. All contents, prerequisite maps, and departmental survival guides are systematically audited and refreshed specifically for incoming **Batch 126 (frosh)**, shiftees, and off-track science students.\n*   **Format & Medium:** Centralized Google Drive repository, dynamic Google Sheets trackers, infographic carousel series on FB/IG, and full integration into the H2Zero.ai local resource ecosystem.\n*   **Scope:** Minimum of 10 guide materials (documents, sheets, or pub releases) produced and updated per term.\n*   **Project Leads:** Pauline S. Galias (President), Director for Academics, Director for Student Services.\n*   **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds.\n\n## COS Bulletin Board (Continued from AY 2025–2026)\n\n*   **Description:** Originally initiated under the AY 2025–2026 administration (Clark Cuaresma) under ARC code `G-SCG-25260016`, the COS Bulletin Board is a centralized real-time information dissemination initiative utilizing a dedicated Telegram channel, social media, and H2Zero.ai. Continued and expanded by President Pauline S. Galias for AY 2026–2027, the bulletin board serves three vital functions: (1) multi-departmental announcement broadcast from USG, SCG, and College Departments, (2) promotion and participation drives for college initiatives, and (3) rapid-response student assistance ensuring timely, verified updates reach all science students, particularly incoming frosh (Batch 126).\n*   **Format & Medium:** Dedicated Telegram broadcast channel, cross-posted Facebook/Instagram announcements, and local sync on H2Zero.ai.\n*   **Deliverables:** Minimum of 100+ Telegram subscribers in Term 1; 100% of major SCG/departmental notices broadcast within 24 hours of release.\n*   **Project Leads:** Pauline S. Galias (President), Director for Documentations, Chief Communications.\n*   **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds.\n\n## Research Grants & Funding Portal\n\n*   **Description:** A working directory of every internal and external grant available, so students are never left guessing where to look for money.\n\n## Research Job Board\n\n*   **Description:** Professors and researchers, both inside DLSU and abroad, can post real research and apprenticeship openings here, giving students without existing connections an actual way in.\n\n## Research Readiness Survey\n\n*   **Description:** A short, recurring check asking students if they feel prepared for research work, so the council knows when to push for better orientation, and a consensus whether RESAP could be needed, instead of assuming, sending them directly through the administration.\n\n## Scholarship Grade Calculator\n\n*   **Description:** Lets scholars calculate exactly what grades they need to keep their scholarship, using the real criteria of their specific scholarship.\n\n## Academic Pathing Tool\n\n*   **Description:** A flowchart that can be interacted with, that shows students exactly what happens to their course load if they fail a class, and gives them an alternate path forward they request to bring straight to the administration for options.\n\n## Syllabus Transparency Portal (Approved by the Administration)\n\n*   **Description:** A standard syllabus format required for every class, so students wanting to study in advance could do so.\n\n## Note Generator\n\n*   **Description:** Even with good intentions such as studying, using AI online can still damage our environment. This is A free tool to convert your notes to flashcards, using locally-run, meaning, no environmental damage AI models, so reviewing for an exam doesn't depend on being able to afford a tutor, nor affecting our environment. Data is also yours, not used by datacenters.\n\n## Free Software Directory\n\n*   **Description:** A list of free, legitimate alternatives to the expensive software students are often buying for their hobbies or academics.\n\n## LaTeX Tutorials & Seminar Series (Math & Physics Edition)\n\n*   **Description:** A specialized scientific typesetting initiative tailored for College of Science students, particularly Mathematics and Physics majors. Features self-paced online modules on H2Zero.ai covering equation syntax, proof environments, matrices, thesis templates, and Overleaf collaboration, complemented by an interactive, peer-led hands-on masterclass.\n*   **Live Seminar:** *LaTeX Essentials: Scientific Typesetting Seminar (Math & Physics Edition)*\n    *   **Date:** November 11, 2026 (Wednesday, Week 6 / Pre-Midterms)\n    *   **Venue:** Campus Lecture Room (Velasco / Andrew Hall) with digital recording archived on the Hub\n    *   **Partners:** Math Circle / Physics Society (PhySoc)\n*   **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds (peer-taught, utilizing campus classrooms and free TeX Live / Overleaf community licenses).\n\n## NMAT Online Repository\n\n*   **Description:** A collection of guides, academic notes, and shared experiences among real lasallians who took NMAT to give tips on how to ace the exam."},{"id":"project-2","folderName":"Project_2_Semana_ng_Siyensya","name":"Project 2 Semana ng Siyensya","faq":"# Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Where exactly do the merchandise profits go?\n\nEvery single peso of profit from the Department Merchandise Drive is directly injected into the Financial Transparency Ledger. The funds are split between the Food Security Pantry to restock essential goods and the Emergency Micro-Grant to provide immediate cash assistance to scholars in need.\n\n### Are the academic incentives guaranteed for Wednesday's competitions?\n\nThe council is actively pushing a formal resolution to the Dean's Office to secure official plus-points or excused absences for participants and winners. We will not announce incentives until we have written approval from the Department Chairs to ensure no student's time is wasted.\n\n### How are the Discord Gaming Night brackets monitored?\n\nThe brackets are strictly scheduled for Saturday to ensure they do not bleed into your Sunday rest day before Midterms. Student volunteer marshals will enforce match start times and strictly penalize delays to keep the event running efficiently.","description":"# Project Description\n\nOne week, two events, and a merchandise drive that funds everything. Wednesday is academic competitions: Quiz Bee, On the Spot Research Making, and a Scientific Terminologies Spelling Bee, with faculty coordination underway for possible academic incentives. Saturday is the COS Discord Gaming Night, with Valorant, Deadlock, and MLBB brackets for students who want a night outside the lab. Department merch for Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Math, and Statistics runs the full week, with all proceeds going to the Food Security Pantry and Emergency Micro-Grant.","executionPlan":"## **Project 2: Semana ng Siyensya**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** All three sub-events are Bucket A. Academic Competitions need a General Pre-Activity (APS) form with a 4-6 day lead time. The Gaming Night is a Tournament, which routes through SLIFE (5-8 day lead time), not APS. The Merchandise Drive needs a Special Approval Slip (SCT) with a 1-3 day lead time. Every promotional material across the week goes through P&M clearance first (24 hours). Note: all financial transactions in this project require specimen signatures on file with OTREAS and the Treasurer's Exam passed by Dir. Finance. See Project 6 pre-launch checklist. Nothing moves without both.\n\nIf the Gaming Night runs a paid entry bracket — teams pay to join, winner takes a portion of the pool — here is how that processes financially. Entry fees collected from participants go into the DF via Deposit Slip (14-day lead time after the last transaction). The prize payout to the winning team is processed under GL Code 519004001 (Tokens, Awards and Prices). Net proceeds after the prize payout sit in the DF as fundraising income. If net profit after all expenses exceeds ₱5,000, a full FRA Report is required within one week of the event's conclusion. Prize recipients must be identified by full name and DLSU ID number on the payment document.\n\n**Recommended event window: March 9-15.** This clears Midterms (Feb 16-21), ILW (Mar 2-7), and lands comfortably before the Activity Ban (Mar 30). It also gives the most runway for faculty coordination and merchandise production without competing with exam season.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Overall Event Coordination | Chief of Operations | President | Chief of Staff | All Directors |\n| Academic Competitions (Wed) | Dir. Academics | Chief of Staff | Dir. Logistics | Dir. Documentations |\n| Faculty Incentive Coordination | Dir. Academics | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Merchandise Design | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| Merchandise Pre-Order & Sales | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Creatives | President |\n| SCT Processing (Merchandise) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Documentations | President |\n| Discord Gaming Night (Sat) | Dir. EXT/INT | Chief Communications | Dir. Logistics | Dir. Documentations |\n| Venue Booking (both events) | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. Academics | Dir. Documentations |\n| All Pre-Act Submissions | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Operations | Dir. Logistics | All Directors |\n| All P\\&M and Promotion | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Documentations |\n| Budget Tracking and Proceeds | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | President | Chief of Staff |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-8 Weeks (8 Weeks Before Event Week)**\n\nFaculty coordination starts here. This is the longest dependency and the one most likely to slip if left late. Dir. Academics sends formal letters to department chairs proposing academic incentives for Wednesday's competitions. The exact form of incentive (extra credit, class standing points, attendance consideration) depends on each professor, so starting early gives time for back-and-forth without pressure.\n\nDir. Finance also draws up a budget draft at this point, covering projected merchandise production costs, venue fees, and a conservative sales estimate for the pre-order system.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nVenue booking for both Wednesday and Saturday goes to Dir. Logistics. Per the handover notes, college approval must be secured at minimum 30 days out. Going at 6 weeks gives a buffer if the first venue falls through.\n\nDir. Creatives starts working on five department merchandise designs: Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Math, and Statistics. Designs need to be final before pre-orders open, not after.\n\n**T-5 Weeks**\n\nIf merch production costs will exceed ₱10,000, Dir. Finance files the SPF at this point — one week before any supplier transaction — with quotations attached. GForm pre-order responses must be attached to the SPF per OTREAS requirements. The SCT at T-4 is separate.\n\nPre-order system opens once designs are finalized. A pre-order model is non-negotiable here. It prevents overstocking, guarantees profit margins, and lets Dir. Finance forecast actual proceeds before production costs are committed.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nDir. Documentations submits the APS Pre-Act for the Academic Competitions (4-6 day lead time) and the SLIFE pre-activity for the Gaming Night Tournament (5-8 day lead time) at the same time. They are separate submissions even though they go in the same week. At 4 weeks out, there is plenty of room to resubmit if something comes back with corrections, without risking a blackout window overlap.\n\nDir. Finance submits the SCT for merchandise sales at the same time.\n\nDir. EXT/INT sets up the Discord server infrastructure for the gaming night. Bracket systems for Valorant, Deadlock, MLBB, and Genshin need to be decided and tested before registration opens.\n\n**T-3 Weeks**\n\nPre-Act approvals should be back by now. If any come back rejected, Dir. Documentations flags it immediately to the Chief of Operations and resubmits same day.\n\nGaming Night registration opens publicly. Dir. Creatives pushes the first promotional materials after P&M clears.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nMajor promotion push across all channels. Pre-order deadline set at this point so production can start with confirmed numbers.\n\nDir. Academics confirms which faculty have agreed to incentivize participation and communicates this in the event promotion so students know about it before deciding to join.\n\n**T-1 Week**\n\nMerchandise in production or confirmed for pickup. Dir. Finance confirms proceeds projection and reports to President.\n\nFinal headcounts for Academic Competitions and Gaming Night. Dir. Logistics confirms all equipment, tables, and setup needs are in place for both venues.\n\n**Event Week: March 9-15**\n\n*   Monday to Friday: Merchandise Drive runs all week at a visible, high-traffic location.\n*   Wednesday: Academic Competitions (Quiz Bee, On the Spot Research Making, Science Terminologies Spelling Bee).\n*   Saturday: COS Discord Gaming Night.\n\nWednesday and Saturday are also when merch gets the hardest promotional push since foot traffic is highest on both days.\n\n**T+5 Days (Post-Event)**\n\nDir. Finance tabulates total proceeds and posts the split to the Transparency Ledger publicly: one portion to the Emergency Micro-Grant Fund, one to the Food Security Pantry. The Ledger update goes out after FAO confirms all receipts are processed, not when the event closes. Same sequencing as P6 and P8. Dir. Documentations submits Post-Activity (ADM) forms within the 5-day window.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\nEvent week of March 9-15 clears all calendar constraints. ILW ends March 7. Activity Ban starts March 30. Pre-Act submissions at T-4 weeks fall in the first week of February, well outside Midterms.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **Faculty confirmation for academic incentives:** No incentive can be advertised until at least two or three professors have confirmed in writing. Dir. Academics chases this from T-8 weeks. If no faculty confirms by T-4 weeks, the promotions still go out, just without the incentive angle. Do not delay promotion waiting for a confirmation that might not come.\n    \n2.  **Venue approval from college (minimum 30 days out):** Covers both the Wednesday competition venue and the Saturday gaming setup. If physical space for the gaming night is unavailable, the Saturday event defaults to fully online through Discord, which needs to be a pre-decided fallback, not a last-minute pivot.\n    \n3.  **Merchandise designs finalized before pre-orders open:** Dir. Creatives cannot hand off to the pre-order system with placeholder designs. Pre-orders open once all five department designs are confirmed and approved by Pauline.\n    \n4.  **APS Pre-Acts cleared before any event-specific promotion goes live:** P&M clearance for general awareness posts can happen earlier. But event-specific promotional materials (with a confirmed date, venue, and mechanic) need the Pre-Acts approved first.\n    \n5.  **SCT for merchandise processed before sales begin:** The Drive cannot open without it. Dir. Finance files this at T-4 weeks alongside the Pre-Acts.\n    \n6.  **Proceeds posted publicly within one week of the event:** Dir. Finance gives Dir. Documentations the final figures. Dir. Creatives posts the proceeds summary on the Transparency Ledger and on social media. This is a public accountability moment, not an internal memo.","subprojects":"# Project 2: Semana ng Siyensya Sub-Projects\n\n## Academic Competitions (Wednesday)\n\n*   **Description:** Quiz Bee, On the Spot Research Making, and Science Terminologies Spelling Bee. Open to all COS students, with possible academic incentives being coordinated with faculty.\n\n## Department Merchandise Drive (All Week)\n\n*   **Description:** Five department lines covering Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Math, and Statistics. All proceeds go directly to the micro-grant and disaster relief pools.\n\n## COS Discord Gaming Night (Saturday)\n\n*   **Description:** Valorant, Deadlock, and MLBB, Genshin competitions and co-op for COS students who want one night that has nothing to do with a deadline."},{"id":"project-3","folderName":"Project_3_Transparency_Platform","name":"Project 3 Transparency Platform","faq":"# Transparency & Accountability Platform — FAQ\n\n**Q: Why should I trust this is actually free?** A: The platform is built using free, open-source technology. Not a single peso of council funds goes into hosting or maintenance—all council money is strictly reserved for emergency relief and research grants, which you can verify yourself in real-time on our Financial Transparency Ledger.\n\n**Q: What stops the council from just deleting negative feedback from the Suggestion Tab?** A: Our explicit moderation policy forbids deleting substantive policy criticism. Posts are only removed if they contain personal harassment or hate speech against identifiable individuals. Furthermore, any removal requires two officers to jointly sign off, and the deletion action is logged internally.\n\n**Q: What if the admin completely ignores a grievance filed through your channel?** A: We will not lie to you: the council cannot force the administration to act. However, we can make their inaction visible. With your explicit consent, cases where the administration ignores a mediation outcome will be published on our public Ledger to apply direct visibility pressure.\n\n**Q: You're demanding transparency from the admin, but how do we know the council isn't hiding its own mistakes?** A: We hold ourselves to the exact same standard. Any grievance filed against an SCG officer bypasses the council entirely and goes straight to an external faculty ombudsperson or OSA. Furthermore, every council expenditure over ₱500 is publicly posted within 72 hours, naming the exact officer who approved it.","description":"# Project Description\n\nA platform that holds the council to the same standard it asks of the administration.","executionPlan":"## **Project 3: Transparency & Accountability Platform**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** All four sub-features are Bucket B for ongoing operations. The initial platform launch is filed as \"Others\" under the DAAM Types of Activity and processes as a General Pre-Activity (APS) with a 4-6 day lead time before any public rollout. After that clears, individual feature announcements each go through P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) and GOSM posting clearance before going public. The Grievance Channel has one additional dependency that every other sub-feature does not: an external mediator must be identified and confirmed before it goes live.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Sub-Feature | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Centralized Grievance Channel | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff, Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| External Mediator Coordination | Chief of Staff | President | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Financial Transparency Ledger | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Student Rights Charter | Chief of Staff | President | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Suggestion Tab | Dir. Student Services | President | Dir. Creatives | Dir. Documentations |\n| Moderation Policy (Suggestion Tab) | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | All Directors |\n| P\\&M for all sub-features | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| All clearance and submission tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff |  |  |\n| Ledger updates (ongoing) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations |  | President |\n| Grievance case mediation | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-6 Weeks (Before Term Start)**\n\nChief of Staff begins identifying the external mediator for the Grievance Channel. This is the most sensitive dependency in the entire platform. The mediator must be either a faculty ombudsperson or an OSA representative, and they need to agree in writing before the channel accepts a single submission. Without this, the Grievance Channel does not open. Dir. Student Services drafts the initial reach-out letter. Pauline signs it.\n\nAt the same time, Chief of Staff drafts the Student Rights Charter. This is a document, not a digital tool, so it does not need web development time. It does need to be reviewed carefully for accuracy before it goes anywhere near a student. Dir. Student Services reviews the draft against actual DLSU student handbook provisions so nothing in the charter contradicts or overpromises what the university's own rules already guarantee.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nExternal mediator confirmation should be in hand by now. If it is not, Chief of Staff escalates to Pauline and the two of them pursue it directly. The Grievance Channel launch date moves with this confirmation, not ahead of it.\n\nDir. Finance sets up the Transparency Ledger structure. The ledger needs a clear, consistent format before it goes public: date, item, amount, approving officer, status. Dir. Finance also establishes the internal rule that any expenditure over ₱500 gets posted within 72 hours of approval. This rule goes into the Comms Protocol so it is not optional.\n\nDir. Student Services drafts the Suggestion Tab moderation policy. Two things need to be decided before launch: what qualifies as a personal attack that gets moderated (and therefore requires two-officer sign-off to remove), and what counts as policy criticism that stays up regardless of how uncomfortable it is. This gets written down, not kept informal.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nDir. Documentations submits the General Pre-Activity (APS) for the platform launch (4-6 day lead time). This goes in before P&M so the two clear around the same time and the launch does not get held up waiting on one of them.\n\nDir. Creatives submits batched P&M clearances for all four sub-features. Student Rights Charter goes through a final read by Chief of Staff and Dir. Student Services before it is handed to Dir. Creatives for design.\n\nWeb platform build is finalized. Dir. Student Services and Dir. Finance do a full walkthrough of the Grievance Channel and Ledger before anything goes public. The Suggestion Tab is tested with internal submissions first to confirm the reply workflow actually works end to end.\n\n**T-1 Week**\n\nExternal mediator agreement is confirmed and filed with Dir. Documentations. This document needs to exist before the channel opens. Dir. Documentations logs it in the clearance tracker.\n\nAll P&M clearances should be back by now. GOSM posting clearance requested.\n\n**Week 1 of Term: Platform Launch**\n\nAll four features go live together. Launching them separately creates confusion about what the platform actually is. The Ledger goes live first, on Day 1, because it starts generating entries the moment the council makes any financial decision. The Grievance Channel, Rights Charter, and Suggestion Tab follow by Day 3 once GOSM clears.\n\n**Ongoing Operations**\n\n*   **Within 72 hours of any expenditure over ₱500:** Dir. Finance posts to the Ledger with the approving officer named. No exceptions, including during Midterms and ILW. The platform stays operational through all blackout windows since it is not an activity.\n*   **Weekly:** Dir. Student Services checks the Suggestion Tab for new submissions. Every submission gets a reply. If a student wants to lead their own idea, Dir. Student Services brings it to Pauline directly.\n*   **Per grievance filed:** Dir. Student Services logs it, loops in the external mediator, and keeps the student informed of status. If the grievance is against an SCG officer, it goes to the external mediator immediately without passing through the council first.\n*   **End of Term:** Dir. Finance reconciles the Ledger and publishes a term summary. Dir. Student Services compiles a Grievance and Suggestion report for Pauline: how many filed, how many resolved, how many suggestions received a response.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\nThis platform is not event-based. It runs through Midterms, ILW, and the Activity Ban without interruption. The only things that pause during blackout windows are promotional pushes and new feature announcements.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **External mediator confirmed in writing before the Grievance Channel opens:** This is the only hard gate in this project. No workaround. Dir. Student Services and Chief of Staff pursue this from T-6 weeks. If the mediator is a faculty member, Chief of Staff coordinates through the appropriate department chair. If it is OSA, Pauline initiates that relationship directly.\n    \n2.  **Moderation policy written and approved before the Suggestion Tab opens:** Dir. Student Services drafts it, Pauline approves it. It needs to exist as a document before the tab goes live so that any moderation decision made in the future has a written basis, not just a judgment call in the moment.\n    \n3.  **Student Rights Charter reviewed against the actual DLSU student handbook:** Chief of Staff and Dir. Student Services do this together. The charter cannot promise students rights that DLSU's own policies do not actually support. Anything that goes beyond existing policy needs to be framed as an advocacy position, not a guarantee.\n    \n4.  **72-hour Ledger rule formalized in the Comms Protocol:** Dir. Finance cannot be the only person enforcing this. It needs to be a written internal rule so that if Dir. Finance is unavailable, the Chief of Operations or Chief of Staff can post in their place without waiting for permission.\n    \n5.  **P&M clearance before any public announcement:** Dir. Creatives batches all four sub-features in one submission at T-2 weeks to avoid a staggered rollout that makes the platform look incomplete.\n    \n6.  **All platform features tested internally before launch:** Dir. Student Services runs a closed test of the Grievance Channel reply workflow and the Suggestion Tab notification system. If something breaks in testing, it gets fixed before students see it.","subprojects":"# Project 3: Transparency & Accountability Platform Sub-Projects\n\n## Centralized Grievance Channel\n\n*   **Description:** A private, secure way to report unfair grading or misconduct, with the council mediating directly and keeping every report confidential.\n\n## Financial Transparency Ledger\n\n*   **Description:** A public, regularly updated record of exactly where every peso of council funds goes.\n\n## Student Rights Charter\n\n*   **Description:** A clear, plain language guide to what every student can ask for, including grade breakdowns in any point of the term and the right to formally dispute a grade, among other things.\n\n## Suggestion Tab\n\n*   **Description:** A live, public space on the dashboard where any student can post an idea. The council reviews and replies to every single one, and if a student wants to head their own idea, we'll make room for it."},{"id":"project-4","folderName":"Project_4_Inclusion_Accessibility","name":"Project 4 Inclusion Accessibility","faq":"# Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation Initiative — FAQ\n\n**Q: Why should I trust this is actually free?** A: The Women and Minority in STEM Archive and the Laboratory Accessibility Audit require zero external funding. The FSL Training Program is fully funded by the council's internal operational budget to cover the honorarium for the professional instructors from our partner Deaf community organization. No fees are charged to students.\n\n**Q: What if someone submits fake achievements to the Women and Minority Archive?** A: The archive is strictly vetted. No profile or achievement is published without explicit, documented consent from the individual, along with verification of their academic standing or alumni status. We strictly comply with the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173).\n\n**Q: What happens if the administration completely ignores the Laboratory Accessibility Audit?** A: Our formal letter to the administration includes a strict 90-day response deadline. If the administration fails to provide a substantive response or a plan to fix the physical barriers we identify, the council will publish the full audit report publicly on the Transparency Platform to force accountability.\n\n**Q: Isn't an FSL program performative if our curriculum doesn't accommodate Deaf students anyway?** A: Systemic change has to start somewhere. Making our student leaders and peers conversational in FSL is the critical first step toward demonstrating demand and building the groundwork for broader, systemic curricular changes in the future. We cannot advocate for inclusion without practicing it ourselves.","description":"# Project Description\n\nThree actions built around one idea, that science spaces should actually work for the people inside them.","executionPlan":"## **Project 4: Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation Initiative**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** Mixed bucket. The Laboratory Accessibility Audit walkthrough and the FSL Training Program are Bucket A. The audit requires a formal Letter and Request (2-3 day lead time). The FSL Training Program requires a General Pre-Activity APS form (4-6 days), and a SLIFE form (5-8 days) since it is a Tie-Up Activity with an external organization, which routes through SLIFE, not APS alone. The Women and Minority in STEM Archive is Bucket B for ongoing operations. Its initial launch as a public-facing website is filed as \"Others\" under the DAAM Types of Activity and processes as a General Pre-Activity (APS) with a 4-6 day lead time. Dir. Documentations files this before the first profile goes live.\n\nOne thing to flag upfront: the FSL Training Program cannot move forward until the External MOA with a Deaf community organization is signed. That MOA has a 14-day DAAM lead time on top of the time needed to actually negotiate it. This is the longest dependency in this project and it needs to start before anything else.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| FSL Training Program | Dir. Advocacy | President | Dir. EXT/INT, Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| FSL Partner MOA Negotiation | Dir. EXT/INT | President | Dir. Advocacy, Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| MOA DAAM Submission | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. EXT/INT | President |\n| Laboratory Accessibility Audit | Dir. Advocacy | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Audit Access Request Letter | Chief of Staff | President | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n| Audit Report Writing | Dir. Advocacy | President | Chief of Staff | All Directors |\n| Audit Report Submission to Admin | President | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| 90-Day Response Tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Advocacy | President |\n| Women and Minority in STEM Archive | Dir. Advocacy | President | Dir. Creatives | Dir. Documentations |\n| Consent Form for Archive | Dir. Advocacy | Chief of Staff |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| P\\&M for all public-facing items | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| APS and SLIFE Pre-Act Submissions | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Advocacy |  |\n| SLIFE Coordination (FSL) | Dir. EXT/INT | Chief Communications | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-10 Weeks (Before FSL Training Target Date)**\n\nDir. EXT/INT starts identifying and reaching out to Deaf community organizations in Metro Manila. The goal is to find a partner with formal FSL instruction capacity, not just a willing volunteer. Two or three options should be in conversation at once so that if one falls through, the MOA process does not start from scratch.\n\nDir. Advocacy reaches out to potential STEM Archive nominees at the same time. Graduating and recently shifted students do not stay reachable indefinitely. The earlier the consent forms go out, the better the response rate.\n\n**T-8 Weeks**\n\nA partner organization is selected. Dir. EXT/INT begins MOA negotiations with them in coordination with Dir. Advocacy. The MOA needs to cover at minimum: training frequency, number of sessions, scope of participants (students, faculty, or both), and who provides the trainer.\n\nChief of Staff drafts the formal access request letter for the Laboratory Accessibility Audit, addressed to the Dean's Office and Physical Facilities Management. Pauline signs it. The letter needs to include a proposed schedule for the walkthrough and a stated 90-day response deadline for the administration's action on the findings.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nMOA draft is finalized and submitted to DAAM. The 14-day external MOA lead time starts here. Dir. Documentations tracks the submission and flags any corrections immediately.\n\nDir. Advocacy sends the access request letter to the Dean's Office and PFM. The 90-day clock on the administration's response starts the moment it is delivered and acknowledged. Dir. Documentations logs the delivery date and sets a calendar reminder for the 90-day mark.\n\nDir. Advocacy also finalizes the consent form for the STEM Archive. It should cover: what information is shared, how it is displayed, how long it stays up, and how a graduate can request removal. Nothing goes on the archive without a signed form.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nMOA should be back from DAAM by now. If it comes back with corrections, Dir. EXT/INT and Dir. Documentations turn it around same day.\n\nDir. Documentations submits the APS Pre-Activity form for the FSL Training Program. If the partner organization qualifies as a social engagement or cross-org activity, the SLIFE form goes in at the same time (5-8 day lead time). Both go in together so they clear at roughly the same time.\n\nDir. Advocacy drafts the Lab Audit walkthrough schedule. The team doing the audit should include Dir. Advocacy, at least one faculty ally to facilitate access, and ideally one student with a disability who can speak directly to what barriers feel like in practice, not just what they look like on paper.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nPre-Acts and SLIFE form should be approved. If anything is still pending, Dir. Documentations escalates to Chief of Staff.\n\nDir. Creatives submits P&M clearance for the FSL Training Program announcement and the STEM Archive launch. Both can be submitted together.\n\nFirst batch of STEM Archive consent forms comes back. Dir. Advocacy reviews each one before any profile goes live. Consent is specific: if a graduate said yes to a photo but no to their thesis title being listed, that distinction is respected exactly.\n\n**FSL Training Program (Target: Weeks 2-4 of Term, Post-GOSM Defense)**\n\nSessions are scheduled based on the MOA terms. If the program covers both students and faculty, run separate sessions rather than mixing them, since faculty and students interact differently with material in a shared classroom setting. Dir. Advocacy coordinates attendance. Dir. Documentations files the AET feedback form within 7 days of each session.\n\n**Laboratory Accessibility Audit (Target: Week 2-3 of Term)**\n\nThe walkthrough happens once access is confirmed by the Dean's Office and PFM. Dir. Advocacy leads the team through every laboratory on the agreed list. Every barrier found is documented with a description, a photograph, and a note on which student population it affects. The report is not a suggestion. It is a formal submission to the Dean's Office and PFM with an explicit request for remediation and a reference to the 90-day response deadline already established in the original access request letter.\n\nIf the 90-day deadline passes with no response, Dir. Advocacy and Chief of Staff bring it to Pauline. The report goes public. This is not a threat held in reserve. It is a stated procedure written into the submission letter from the start.\n\n**Women and Minority in STEM Archive (Target: Week 1 of Term)**\n\nThe archive can go live as soon as the first batch of consented profiles is ready and P&M clears. It does not need to wait for a full roster. Starting with even five or six profiles is stronger than waiting three months for a complete list. Dir. Advocacy adds new profiles on a rolling basis throughout the term as more consent forms come in. Dir. Documentations submits the General Pre-Activity (APS) for the archive website launch before the first profile goes up — filed as \"Others\" since it is a public-facing website. P&M follows after.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\n*   FSL Training sessions should not be scheduled during Midterms (Feb 16-21) or ILW (Mar 2-7). If the program runs multiple sessions, schedule them in Weeks 2-5 and then again post-ILW.\n*   The Lab Audit walkthrough is not a student activity in the DAAM sense, but scheduling it during Midterms creates unnecessary friction. Target Week 2-3.\n*   Archive updates are a standing platform feature and continue through all blackout windows without issue.\n*   MOA submission at T-6 weeks should fall in early January, well before any blackout periods.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **FSL partner MOA signed and DAAM-cleared before any FSL session is announced publicly:** Dir. EXT/INT starts this at T-10 weeks. The program does not get promoted until the MOA clears. A named partner organization in the promotion is more credible and more defensible than a vague announcement.\n    \n2.  **Dean's Office and PFM access confirmation before the Lab Audit walkthrough:** The letter goes out at T-6 weeks. If access is denied, Chief of Staff escalates to Pauline and the two of them pursue it directly at the administrative level. The audit does not get cancelled because of an unresponsive office. It gets delayed until access is granted, and that delay is documented.\n    \n3.  **Faculty ally confirmed for the audit team:** Dir. Advocacy identifies this person before the walkthrough is scheduled. A faculty presence makes the visit harder for building management to dismiss or delay.\n    \n4.  **Consent forms returned and reviewed before any profile goes live on the STEM Archive:** Dir. Advocacy owns this review. No profile goes up without explicit written consent, no matter how public the person's achievements already are.\n    \n5.  **APS and SLIFE Pre-Acts cleared before FSL program promotion goes public:** Dir. Documentations submits both at T-4 weeks. Dir. Creatives holds all event-specific P&M until both come back approved.\n    \n6.  **90-day response deadline documented from day of audit report delivery:** Dir. Documentations logs the exact delivery date. Chief of Staff sets a calendar alert. This date is not approximate. The 90-day follow-up procedure is triggered on that exact day if no substantive response has come.","subprojects":"# Project 4: Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation Initiative Sub-Projects\n\n## Filipino Sign Language Training Program\n\n*   **Description:** Formal FSL training for students and faculty, so the Deaf and hard of hearing community isn't an afterthought in our classrooms and labs.\n\n## Laboratory Accessibility Audit\n\n*   **Description:** A real, student led review of every laboratory to find physical barriers for students with disabilities, with a formal report submitted to the administration for fixing.\n\n## Women and Minority in STEM\n\n*   **Description:** A public record and posting of the academic and professional achievements of women and minority graduates in this college, so current students have proof, not just encouragement, that they belong here."},{"id":"project-5","folderName":"Project_5_Queer_Trans_Education","name":"Project 5 Queer Trans Education","faq":"# Queer & Trans Identity Education Series — FAQ\n\n**Q: Why should I trust this is actually free?** A: The educational forums require minimal funding, strictly for tokens of appreciation for our academic speakers, which is covered by the existing council budget. The Shanghay Laya website is built with open-source tools. No fees are charged to students for any part of this initiative.\n\n**Q: What if the Shanghay Laya website provides dangerous medical advice?** A: Shanghay Laya is explicitly a directory, not a medical provider. It features a legally reviewed disclaimer stating this fact. The hormone-resource section strictly links _only_ to licensed endocrinologists and established clinics. We completely prohibit links to informal sources or DIY hormone guides to ensure complete safety and legality.\n\n**Q: What if the administration tries to censor the Science Forum or block the event?** A: The forum is strictly grounded in peer-reviewed biological and sociological science, led by credentialed academics. If the administration attempts to block rigorous academic discourse, we will leverage our faculty partners and the Student Rights Charter to defend our fundamental right to academic freedom.\n\n**Q: Why is the council wasting time on a \"niche minority issue\" instead of things that affect everyone?** A: Science is objective, yet queer and trans students face unscientific prejudice every day in our classrooms. Clarifying the science behind our own identities isn't a niche issue; it's a defense of our community's right to exist safely and unapologetically on campus. Inclusivity affects the integrity of the entire college.","description":"# Project Description\n\nThree sessions, one grounded in current science and one grounded in Philippine history, built to make one point clearly: queer and trans Filipinos have always been part of this community.","executionPlan":"## **Project 5: Queer & Trans Identity Education Series**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** Mixed bucket. The Queer Identity Science Forum and the Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session are both Bucket A, each requiring a General Pre-Activity APS form (4-6 day lead time). Given that all speakers must hold academic positions or peer-reviewed credentials per this plan's own standard, both sessions will almost certainly qualify as Seminars/Workshops with Distinguished Speakers, routing through SLIFE (5-8 days). Plan for this as a certainty. Confirm with DAAM before assuming APS alone covers it. Shanghay Laya is Bucket B for ongoing operations. The site launch is filed as \"Others\" under the DAAM Types of Activity and processes as a General Pre-Activity (APS) with a 4-6 day lead time. Dir. Documentations files this alongside the legal disclaimer review, not after it. However, Shanghay Laya carries a legal dependency that no other Bucket B feature in this platform has: a legally reviewed disclaimer must be in place before the site goes live. That review is a hard gate, not a suggestion.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Queer Identity Science Forum | Dir. Advocacy | President | Chief of Staff, Dir. National Affairs | Dir. Documentations |\n| Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session | Dir. Advocacy | President | Dir. National Affairs, Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Speaker Identification & Vetting | Dir. Advocacy | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| SLIFE Coordination (External Speakers) | Dir. EXT/INT | Chief Communications | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n| APS and SLIFE Pre-Act Submissions | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Advocacy |  |\n| Shanghay Laya Website | Dir. Advocacy | President | Dir. Creatives, Chief Communications | Dir. Documentations |\n| Legal Disclaimer Review | Chief of Staff | President | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n| Hormone Resource Vetting | Dir. Advocacy | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| P\\&M for all public-facing items | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n| Venue Booking (both sessions) | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. Advocacy | Dir. Documentations |\n| Post-Activity Report (both sessions) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Advocacy |  |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-10 Weeks (Before Target Session Dates)**\n\nSpeaker identification starts here, and the standard is non-negotiable from day one. Every speaker for both sessions must hold a relevant academic position or have published peer-reviewed research in their field. Dir. Advocacy draws up a shortlist of at minimum three candidates per session so that if one declines or becomes unavailable, the program does not stall.\n\nFor the Science Forum, the search covers researchers in biology, psychology, or sociology with published work on queer identities. For the History Session, the search focuses on Philippine historians or anthropologists with documented work on pre-colonial gender diversity or the Babaylan tradition specifically.\n\nDir. Advocacy also starts building the Shanghay Laya content list at this point, identifying which organizations, clinics, and resources will be linked. Every hormone resource entry gets flagged for the legal disclaimer review before the site goes live.\n\n**T-8 Weeks**\n\nSpeaker confirmations are targeted by this point. Dir. Advocacy secures written confirmation from each speaker, covering session date, format, and scope of presentation. If either speaker is external to DLSU, Dir. EXT/INT begins the SLIFE coordination process immediately since the 5-8 day lead time only counts from submission, not from when the decision to invite was made.\n\nChief of Staff begins the legal disclaimer review for Shanghay Laya. This does not require a licensed lawyer. A law student with sufficient background, reviewed carefully, is enough. What the review needs to confirm: the website is a directory, not a medical provider; every hormone resource link goes to a licensed endocrinologist or LGBTQ+-affirming clinic; and the disclaimer on every page is specific enough to be defensible, not just a generic \"consult your doctor\" line. The review is documented, signed off, and filed with Dir. Documentations before the site goes live.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nDir. Documentations submits APS Pre-Activity forms for both sessions. If external speakers are confirmed, SLIFE forms go in at the same time. Both sessions can be submitted together since they are part of the same project.\n\nDir. Logistics books venues for both sessions. The Science Forum and the History Session are separate events and do not need to happen on the same day, but scheduling them within the same two-week window keeps the project cohesive and the promotional momentum from the first session carries into the second.\n\nDir. Advocacy finalizes all Shanghay Laya content. Every link is checked. Anything that cannot be verified as a licensed or peer-reviewed source does not make it onto the site. The hormone resources section specifically lists only licensed endocrinologists and LGBTQ+-affirming clinics, with each entry reviewed individually by Dir. Advocacy before inclusion.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nPre-Act approvals should be back. If anything is rejected or comes back with corrections, Dir. Documentations flags it to Chief of Staff same day and resubmits.\n\nLegal disclaimer review for Shanghay Laya is completed and signed off. Dir. Advocacy and Chief of Staff both sign the confirmation that the review happened and who conducted it. This document lives in Dir. Documentations' clearance tracker permanently.\n\nDir. Creatives submits P&M clearance for both session announcements. Dir. Documentations submits the General Pre-Activity (APS) for the Shanghay Laya website launch (4-6 day lead time), filed as \"Others,\" at the same time. Both go in together but they are separate submissions going to separate channels.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nPromotion for both sessions goes live once P&M clears. Dir. Creatives leads the rollout across platforms. Promotional materials for the sessions name the speakers and their credentials. This is not just good marketing. It is the first line of defense against any challenge to the academic legitimacy of the content.\n\nShanghay Laya officially launches on **November 7, 2026 (Saturday / Week 5–6)** under the **SCG Online Portal** once the legal disclaimer review is complete and P&M has cleared. The website serves as a permanent, yearlong educational repository and does not wait for the seminar sessions. Students can access directories and research immediately upon launch.\n\n**Session Week (Target: Weeks 3-5 of Term, Post-GOSM Defense)**\n\nThe two sessions are scheduled within a two-week window of each other. The Science Forum runs first since it grounds the academic basis for queer and trans identities. The History Session follows, building on that foundation with pre-colonial context. Running them in this order is intentional: science first, history second, so the narrative arc is clear.\n\nDir. Advocacy manages the room for both sessions. Dir. Documentations has an AET feedback form ready for distribution at the end of each session. Forms are collected before people leave, not sent digitally afterward where response rates drop significantly.\n\nDir. Documentations submits Post-Activity reports for both sessions within 5 days of each event.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\nBoth sessions target Weeks 3-5 of the term. This clears GOSM Defense (Jan 19-24) and lands well before Midterms (Feb 16-21). No sessions are scheduled during Midterms, ILW (Mar 2-7), or the Activity Ban (Mar 30 - Apr 11). Shanghay Laya as a standing platform is unaffected by all blackout windows. Promotion pauses during ILW and Midterms but the site stays live.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **Speaker credentials confirmed in writing before any promotion goes out:** Dir. Advocacy owns this. A speaker's name appears in promotional materials only after their academic position or published research has been verified and documented. No exceptions, regardless of how trusted or well-known the person is within the community.\n    \n2.  **Legal disclaimer review completed and signed off before Shanghay Laya goes live:** Chief of Staff coordinates this. The site does not open without it. If the review is still pending when everything else is ready, the site launch moves, not the review deadline.\n    \n3.  **All hormone resource links verified as licensed providers before site launch:** Dir. Advocacy reviews each entry individually. This is not a task that gets delegated to a committee or done as a batch check. Every link is opened, the provider's license or institutional affiliation is confirmed, and the link is logged in a master list that Dir. Documentations keeps on file.\n    \n4.  **APS Pre-Acts and SLIFE forms cleared before event-specific promotion goes live:** Dir. Documentations submits both at T-6 weeks. P&M clearance for general awareness posts can happen earlier, but date-specific promotional materials wait for Pre-Act approval.\n    \n5.  **Venue secured before session dates are announced publicly:** Dir. Logistics confirms both venues before any date appears in promotional materials. Announcing a date and then changing it because a room fell through undermines the credibility of the whole initiative.\n    \n6.  **AET feedback forms collected in person at the end of each session:** Dir. Documentations prepares physical forms for both events. Digital follow-up is a backup, not the primary collection method.","subprojects":"# Project 5: Queer & Trans Identity Education Series Sub-Projects\n\n## Queer Identity Science Forum\n\n*   **Description:** An academic session on the biological, psychological, and sociological research behind queer identities, taught plainly and without apology.\n\n## Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History\n\n*   **Description:** A look at gender diversity in pre-colonial Philippine history, including the Babaylan, so students understand this isn't a new conversation, it's a returning one.\n\n## Shanghay Laya–Malaya Maging Ikaw, Sa Gitna ng Lahat\n*   **Description:** A specialized educational and health resource website hosted under the SCG Online Portal. Provides vetted medical research on queer and trans health, common myths debunked, directories for licensed endocrinologists and hormone therapy clinics, LGBTQ+-affirming mental health resources, and Philippine pre-colonial gender diversity history.\n*   **Official Platform Launch:** November 7, 2026 (Saturday / Week 5–6)\n*   **Access & Medium:** Integrated within the SCG Online Portal (H2Zero.ai) and distributed via social media awareness campaigns.\n*   **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds (built with open-source tools; zero commercial hosting expenses)."},{"id":"project-6","folderName":"Project_6_Emergency_Response","name":"Project 6 Emergency Response","faq":"# Student Emergency Response & Relief Program: FAQ\n\n**Q: Do I have to prove I need help during a typhoon? Isn't that humiliating?** A: You prove nothing during the typhoon. The registry exists so you never have to. At the start of the term, when there is no storm and no pressure, you fill out a short form. A few sentences about your living situation, not an essay. You check which categories fit: commuter from a flood-prone barangay, dormer whose allowance is already stretched thin, student with a medical condition that needs stable power. You rank yourself. You do it once, privately, when everything is calm. When a warning drops, we use that to reach out directly to you. You don't have to message us and explain your situation while you're scared and stuck. We already know. That's the whole point.\n\n**Q: How do you decide who gets help if the money runs out?** A: Transparently, and before anyone needs it. The prioritization criteria are published with the registry before registration even opens. It's not a council vote during the storm. Students in the highest-risk categories get contacted first: commuters from flood-prone areas, dormers without family nearby, students with compounding medical needs. The scoring looks at three things: how often the student faces this risk, how bad it gets when it hits, and whether multiple risks are stacking. You can read that scoring before you submit. No back-room decisions. And if the fund runs out before everyone is reached, we tell the remaining students directly. You know the balance. You know the order. You know why.\n\n**Q: Where does the starting fund come from, and how much is there?** A: The program opens with ₱10,000. That money came from people who personally believe in this project and are supporting it before any institutional approval comes through. It is not hypothetical. It's already committed. We're reaching out to PUSO and other organizations for more, and if the admin pre-act clears, those funds come in on top. Every peso that enters is posted on the Transparency Ledger from Day 1. The starting balance, every disbursement, whatever's left. All visible to registered students throughout the term.\n\n**Q: What happens if the fund runs out mid-typhoon?** A: We tell you directly. Registered students who haven't been reached yet get a message saying the fund has been fully allocated for this storm cycle. We don't act like it didn't happen. What we commit to alongside that message: pursuing replenishment through P8 proceeds and our partner outreach, and logging every student who applied after it ran out as first priority for the next cycle. You're not denied. You're deferred, and we say that plainly.\n\n**Q: Who decides when the emergency protocols activate?** A: PAGASA decides, not us. The moment an official Tropical Cyclone Wind Signal No. 1 or a Yellow Rainfall Warning is issued for Metro Manila, Phase 1 is on. When that escalates to TCWS No. 2 or an Orange or Red Rainfall Warning, Phase 2 is on. No internal debate. No officer making a call based on how the sky looks outside. The PAGASA bulletin number is the trigger, and any student can verify it themselves. That's what keeps bias out of it.\n\n**Q: Can you actually send GCash during a super typhoon if cell towers are down?** A: We want to be honest about this, because you should know before you're in the middle of it. Sometimes, no. During a bad typhoon, cell towers go down and mobile data drops. If you have no signal when the transfer goes out, it won't reach you in real time. If roads are flooded, delivery apps stop running even if the money gets through. We can't fix the city's infrastructure and we won't pretend we can. But if you registered, the whole point is that you already visited the pantry before it got to that point. The Emergency Relief Grant is for students who are stranded and still have connectivity. It's not a guarantee in every scenario, and we say that from the start.\n\n**Q: What if I need physical food during the typhoon itself?** A: We know how hard that is. Being stuck at home with nothing and watching the water rise outside is genuinely frightening, and we wish we could say we'd find a way to get it to you. We can't. We will not send a student or a council officer through floodwaters for any reason. The Food Security Program closes when moving becomes dangerous. But if you registered and couldn't visit during Phase 1, you are not forgotten. The moment the signal drops and it's safe again, the pantry reopens. We send a check-in message to every registered student letting them know when it opens and what's available. You're first. We just have to wait for it to be safe first.","description":"# Project Description\n\nA weather-triggered Food Security Program and Emergency Relief Grant built on one idea: we reach out to you. You don't have to come to us mid-typhoon. Students who sign up at the start of the term are the ones we contact when the warning drops. The budget is visible. The priority system is published. And we tell you the limits before you're in the middle of needing us.","executionPlan":"## **Project 6: Student Emergency Response & Relief Program**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** Mixed bucket. The Food Security Program is Bucket B for ongoing operations, but needs a Special Approval Slip on top of P&M clearance before any funds move or goods are procured. The Emergency Relief Grant Fund is also Bucket B, with every disbursement cycle processed as a single batched Direct Payment with a formal Letter of Explanation. The Welfare Outreach Registry is a Website/Social Media Launch under DAAM and goes through SLIFE (5-8 day lead time) before the intake form goes public. Check with DAAM whether the registry also qualifies as Survey Dissemination with Sensitive Information, since it collects self-declared vulnerability data, and file whichever classification is more restrictive. Every financial step here is grounded in the 16th USG Treasurer's Manual.\n\nTwo things must happen before any transaction in this project can legally go through. Specimen signatures submitted to OTREAS. And the Treasurer's Exam passed by Dir. Finance. OTREAS locks the account without both. These aren't tasks to get around to. They are the first two items and they block everything else.\n\n### **What This Program Is**\n\nWe don't wait for students to come to us during a typhoon. That is the moment people are least able to ask, most embarrassed to be seen asking, and most likely to hear the fund is already gone.\n\nThe program opens with ₱10,000 in seed funding from people who personally believe in this project and are supporting it before any institutional approval comes in. This is not money that depends on a pre-act clearing. The foundation is already there. We are reaching out to PUSO and partner organizations for more, and if the admin pre-act goes through, those funds add on top of what's already committed.\n\nThe whole system runs on data collected during calm weather. Before any storm is in the forecast, students have already told us their situation. So when PAGASA issues a warning, the decisions are made and the outreach goes directly to the people who signed up.\n\n### **The Welfare Outreach Registry: How It Works**\n\nThe registry is the backbone. Without it, there is no triage system, no priority list, and no way to reach students before conditions get bad. It goes live on Day 1 of term.\n\nThe intake form is short. A few sentences about their living situation, not an essay. Students check boxes for their circumstances and explain in their own words why a storm or flood would hit them hardest. Categories include: commuter from a flood-prone barangay, dormer relying on a monthly family allowance, student with a medical condition that needs stable power or refrigeration, student without family support nearby.\n\nThe scoring looks at three things: how often the student faces this risk, how bad it gets when it happens, and whether multiple risks are compounding on top of each other. Those criteria are published alongside the intake form. Every student who registers can read them before they submit. And all data is opt-in, handled only by elected officers, and wiped at the end of the academic term unless the student explicitly re-consents. RA 10173 compliance is built into the process.\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Welfare Outreach Registry (Setup & Maintenance) | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Registry Scoring & Priority Tiers | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Registry Data Privacy Compliance | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Term-End Data Wipe | Dir. Student Services | Chief of Staff | | Dir. Documentations |\n| Pantry Stock Initial Purchase (Direct Payment, Procurement) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Logistics | President |\n| Pantry Operations & Daily Inventory Logging | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| Pantry Restock Trigger (20% Rule) | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. Finance | Dir. Documentations |\n| PAGASA Monitoring & Phase Activation | Chief of Operations | President | All Directors | Dir. Documentations |\n| Phase 1 Private Outreach to Registry | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Operations | Dir. Documentations |\n| Phase 2 Emergency Relief Grant Disbursements | Dir. Finance | President | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Disbursement Batch LOE Preparation | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Student Services | President |\n| Transparency Ledger Updates (Real-Time & Post-Storm) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | | Dir. Documentations |\n| Specimen Signature Submission to OTREAS | Dir. Finance | President | | Dir. Documentations |\n| Treasurer's Exam | Dir. Finance | President | | Chief of Staff |\n| SCT Processing (Pantry & Grant Fund) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Documentations | President |\n| SLIFE Pre-Act (Registry) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Student Services | |\n| P&M Clearance for all sub-features | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | | Dir. Documentations |\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-8 Weeks (Before Term Start)**\n\nDir. Finance submits specimen signatures to OTREAS and schedules the Treasurer's Exam in the same week. No transaction in this project can be filed without an active OTREAS account. If that's late, the pantry opens empty and there's no buffer before the first storm.\n\nAt the same time, Dir. Student Services drafts the registry intake form and the scoring framework. The criteria have to be final before the form goes live because they're posted alongside the registration link. If they change after the registry opens, the fairness of the whole triage system falls apart.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nTreasurer's Exam cleared. Dir. Finance confirms OTREAS account access before anything else is filed.\n\nDir. Finance files the initial pantry stock purchase as a Direct Payment (Procurement). Filing at T-6 weeks puts goods on the shelf around T-3 weeks, before the term starts and before any storm. The procurement draws from the Operational Fund under GL Code 512201001 (Food and Meals) for rice and canned goods, and GL Code 511001004 (Other Materials and Supplies) for storage materials. Document chain: PRS Table, Direct Payment Memo, List of Expenses, Official Receipt, Approved A-Form, Proof of Approval. Dir. Documentations tracks every step.\n\nDir. Finance also declares the Emergency Relief Grant pool as a ring-fenced allocation within the Depository Fund for the GOSM submission. The floor is set before the term begins, before any income hits the DF. It can't be set after the fact.\n\nOne supplier. One contact. One purchase order. The council buys in bulk and hands off to students directly. No co-procurement with other orgs. A registered student who visits the pantry gets an immediate answer from a council officer, not from a chain of people calling each other.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nDir. Documentations submits the SLIFE pre-activity for the Welfare Registry (5-8 day lead time). Dir. Creatives submits P&M clearances for both the pantry and the registry at the same time so they clear together.\n\nDir. Student Services locks in the internal Phase 2 intake protocol: what a student submits during the strike window, who reviews it, the turnaround time, how the batch LOE is structured. This is written down before any storm arrives. It is not figured out under pressure.\n\nDir. Finance drafts the officer advance protocol memo for Pauline's signature. It's signed before the term starts, not after the first emergency hits.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nPantry stock confirmed or already in. Dir. Logistics does a full count and logs the opening inventory. The 20% restock rule goes into writing now: when stock drops to 20% of the opening count, Dir. Logistics flags Dir. Finance the same day and a new Direct Payment (Procurement) is filed immediately. The 21-day restock clock starts on the flag date, not when the shelf goes empty.\n\nAll SLIFE and P&M clearances back and confirmed. Dir. Documentations logs everything before any public launch.\n\n**Term Start: Program Goes Live**\n\nThe Welfare Outreach Registry opens on Day 1. It gets actively promoted in the first two weeks through GOSM, student channels, and class group chats. Because a registry with a handful of entries in Week 6 isn't a triage system. It needs real numbers before the first storm of the season.\n\nThe pantry opens fully stocked. Dir. Logistics tracks item types and quantities per visit. Student names are not recorded at the pantry. The registry has the names. The visit itself is quiet.\n\nThe Transparency Ledger goes live on Day 1 with the opening pantry inventory and grant fund starting balance posted. Students who signed up can see what's in the program before they ever need it.\n\n### **The Weather Protocol**\n\n**Phase 1: Pre-Storm Window**\nTrigger: Official PAGASA TCWS No. 1 or Yellow Rainfall Warning for Metro Manila.\n\nChief of Operations confirms the bulletin from the official PAGASA source and briefs the President. Dir. Student Services pulls the high-risk tier from the registry and sends them a private message through the contact channel they provided at sign-up. Not a public GOSM post. A direct, quiet message telling them the Food Security Program pantry is open for extended hours and to visit before conditions get worse.\n\nAfter the 12-hour head start window, a general awareness post may go out at Dir. Student Services' discretion. But registered students were already contacted first. That head start is the registry's value. It's why the data is collected during calm weather and not during the storm.\n\nStudent names are not logged at the pantry visit. Inventory quantities are. No decision is made at the door.\n\n**Phase 2: The Strike**\nTrigger: Official PAGASA TCWS No. 2+ or Orange/Red Rainfall Warning for Metro Manila.\n\nThe physical pantry closes. No student should be moving. No council officer goes out to the field.\n\nDir. Student Services opens a direct contact channel sent exclusively to registered students: those who are stranded and need immediate cash for food delivery or medicine. Students send a brief message or fill in a short form explaining their situation. Dir. Student Services reviews it against the registry and confirms eligibility. Dir. Finance processes the Emergency Relief Grant disbursements (₱200-300 per student) via GCash or Maya, and batches all approvals from the same window into a single LOE for the Direct Payment filing.\n\nBecause typhoons give 48 to 72 hours of warning before a signal escalates, Dir. Finance uses that window to pre-file the batch with OTREAS based on projected registry need. The paperwork starts while the storm is still in the Philippine Area of Responsibility. You use the bureaucracy's own timeline against it.\n\nEvery disbursement is posted to the Transparency Ledger in real time. The balance is visible to registered students throughout. When it runs out, that is stated plainly. Students who submitted after the fund ran out are told directly, logged as first priority for the next cycle, and given a replenishment timeline.\n\n**Phase 3: Aftermath**\nTrigger: PAGASA advisory confirming signal reduction or storm exit from Metro Manila.\n\nThe pantry reopens. Priority goes to students who lost refrigerated food to power outages or can't get to a grocery store. Dir. Logistics updates the inventory count. If stock is below the 20% trigger, the restock Direct Payment is filed the same day. Dir. Student Services sends a check-in message to all registered students: pantry is open, here are the hours, here is what's available.\n\nDir. Finance posts the post-storm Transparency Ledger update within 48 hours. Pantry items distributed by type and quantity, how many grants were processed, total spent from the fund, remaining balance. Posted publicly on council platforms. Every student in the college can see how it ran.\n\n### **Transparency Commitments**\n\nThese are published at the start of the term alongside the intake form. Every student who considers registering reads them before submitting.\n\nThe fund balance is visible at all times. Starting balance, each disbursement, what remains, posted as each transaction happens.\n\nThe prioritization criteria are published before the registry opens. Students read the scoring before they submit.\n\nThe limitations are stated at registration. The fund is finite. Physical distribution only happens when it's safe to move. If the fund runs out before everyone is reached, those students are told directly, placed first for the next cycle, and we commit publicly to replenishment.\n\nNo student data is kept past the academic term. Wiped at term end unless the student re-consents.\n\n### **Hard Limitations (Published With the Program)**\n\nThese are stated at registration and defended at the GOSM. Not disclosing them is not an option.\n\n**The Math.** The program starts with ₱10,000 and we're actively raising more through PUSO and partner organizations. Until that comes in, the fund is what it is. The Emergency Relief Grant is a shock-absorber for the most vulnerable registered students, not a school-wide disaster fund. We say this at launch, not after the money is gone.\n\n**The Infrastructure.** During a bad typhoon, cell towers go down. A grant transfer may not reach you in real time if your signal is out. If roads are flooded, delivery services stop running even if the money gets through. We can't fix the city's infrastructure. But if you registered, the point is that you already visited the pantry before it got to that point.\n\n**Physical Safety.** The pantry closes when moving is dangerous. No student gets asked to travel during an active TCWS 2 or above. No council officer goes out to deliver goods through floodwaters. If you didn't visit during Phase 1, you wait for Phase 3. Safety is the rule the whole system is built around.\n\n### **Contingency: If Administration Rejects the Pre-Act or Formal Channel**\n\nWeather doesn't wait for DAAM processing windows. And a program with no backup plan for bureaucratic delay will fail its students the first time an office is slow.\n\n**If the SLIFE for the Welfare Registry is rejected:**\n\nThe registry doesn't need a formal platform to work. If the SLIFE closes, Pauline reaches out personally through her own social media and messaging accounts, with a clear disclaimer on each message that she is speaking as an individual and not as a representative of SCG. She uses Messenger group chats to cover reach. Students who want to register reply directly to her. She sends them the same self-declaration form individually. The same scoring criteria apply. The same data privacy protocol applies. The list looks the same and the system works the same. Only the intake channel changes.\n\n**If the OTREAS account is not active when a storm arrives:**\n\nThe officer advance protocol activates. This is a pre-signed, pre-authorized internal memo between Pauline and Dir. Finance. The President can advance personal funds for same-period emergency disbursements. It is not a loan from Pauline to the student. It is a temporary bridge. Dir. Finance files the Direct Payment Student Reimbursement the same day the advance is made. The formal OTREAS channel catches up within its 21-day window. No student is asked to wait for a clearance while they're stranded.\n\n**If pantry stock procurement is delayed:**\n\nDir. Finance and Dir. Logistics source emergency stock from the nearest wholesale supplier using an officer advance with receipts. The formal procurement is filed on the Direct Payment track at the same time. The pantry does not open without goods on the shelf.\n\n**If the Emergency Relief Grant fund hits zero mid-storm:**\n\nThe Transparency Ledger posts the depletion in real time. The council issues a public statement acknowledging the fund is fully allocated and commits to replenishment through P8 proceeds, PUSO and partner outreach, or reallocation from unspent operational line items. Students who submitted after it ran out are first priority for the next cycle. No student is told they missed the window without being told what comes next.\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1. **Specimen signatures at OTREAS and Treasurer's Exam cleared at T-8 weeks.** Nothing moves without account access. First action, not last.\n\n2. **Registry scoring framework finalized before the intake form opens.** Students read the criteria before submitting. The framework can't be revised after registration opens.\n\n3. **Pantry stock Direct Payment filed at T-6 weeks.** The 21-day lead time is not adjustable. Filing late means an empty shelf on Day 1.\n\n4. **DF ring-fence floor declared in the GOSM before the term starts.** The Emergency Relief Grant pool is in writing before any income hits the DF.\n\n5. **Welfare Registry live and promoted actively from Day 1.** A registry with a handful of entries in Week 6 can't function as triage. Launch on Day 1, promote in Weeks 1 and 2.\n\n6. **Phase activation protocol written and distributed to all officers before term starts.** Every officer knows the PAGASA trigger thresholds, their role in each phase, and who has authority to send the Phase 1 message and close the pantry for Phase 2.\n\n7. **20% restock trigger formalized before the pantry opens.** A standing rule, not a call each time the shelf gets low.\n\n8. **Officer advance memo signed by Pauline and Dir. Finance before term starts.** An unsigned advance is a personal financial risk. The signature makes it a council-backed, reimbursable action.\n\n9. **Post-storm Transparency Ledger update posted within 48 hours of Phase 3.** The public commitment to transparency is what earns the trust that makes the registry work in the first place.","subprojects":"# Project 6: Student Emergency Response & Relief Program Sub-Projects\n\n## Food Security Program\n\n*   **Description:** Pre-stocked with rice, canned goods, and other essentials before the term begins. Registered students get a 12-hour head start to visit when a storm warning drops, before conditions make travel dangerous. The pantry reopens after the storm for students who lost refrigerated food or can't get to a grocery store. Inventory is tracked by item type and quantity, not by student name.\n\n## Emergency Relief Grant Fund\n\n*   **Description:** Small digital transfers via GCash or Maya for registered students who are stranded during an active typhoon warning and need to order food or medicine. Activates only when PAGASA issues TCWS No. 2 or above, or an Orange/Red Rainfall Warning for Metro Manila. The fund opens with ₱10,000 in starting capital from people who personally support this project, with more being sought through PUSO and partner organizations. Every disbursement is posted publicly in real time and the balance is visible to all registered students throughout the storm.\n\n## Welfare Outreach Registry\n\n*   **Description:** A short self-declaration form students fill out at the start of the term. They describe their living situation in a few sentences, check which risk categories fit them, and explain why a storm or flood would hit them hardest. The council uses a published scoring framework to build a priority tier list before any emergency happens. When a warning drops, the council reaches out to registered students directly. Students don't have to ask during a crisis. The registry exists because they already raised their hand during calm weather."},{"id":"project-7","folderName":"Project_7_Financial_Empowerment","name":"Project 7 Financial Empowerment","faq":"# Student Financial Empowerment Initiative — FAQ\n\n**Q: What if the lab equipment or calculator I got from the Exchange doesn't work perfectly?** A: All items on the Equipment & Apparel Exchange are entirely donation-based and come with **no implied warranty**. While our team conducts a basic condition check and labels items as \"Tested\" or \"Untested\", students claim items at their own risk and discretion.\n\n**Q: I have work during the Freelancing Seminar. How can I get the tips?** A: We know working students have the tightest schedules. That is why we explicitly planned to record the live seminar. The full recording and presentation slides will be permanently archived and available on the Student Resource Hub within 48 hours after the event concludes.\n\n**Q: Is the Student Council partnered with or endorsing the freelancing platforms mentioned in the seminar?** A: No. Pauline will be sharing her personal, lived experience as a working student who funds her own degree. The council does not officially endorse, sponsor, or partner with any specific freelancing company, agency, or platform mentioned during her talk.","description":"# Project Description\n\nTwo ways to ease financial pressure without asking anyone for money they don't have.","executionPlan":"## **Project 7: Student Financial Empowerment Initiative**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** Mixed bucket. The Equipment and Apparel Exchange is Bucket B for ongoing operations. Its platform launch is filed as \"Others\" under the DAAM Types of Activity and processes as a General Pre-Activity (APS) with a 4-6 day lead time before the site goes public. Dir. Documentations files this at the same time as the donation drive pre-launch so both clear before anything goes live. The pre-launch donation drive is a Donation Drive under SLIFE (5-8 day lead time). Dir. Documentations files this at the same time as the website launch APS so both clear before anything goes public. The Freelancing and Part-Time Work Seminar is Bucket A, requiring a General Pre-Activity APS form (4-6 days). The seminar is a Career Related project, which routes through OCCS under SLIFE (5-8 day lead time). File the OCCS SLIFE form — do not assume APS alone is sufficient. Dir. Documentations confirms this with DAAM before filing. Both sub-projects are zero-cost. No SCT, no DF involvement, no fundraising compliance requirements.\n\nTwo things define this project before the timeline even starts. First, the Equipment Exchange needs donations before it has anything to offer. A platform with zero listings on launch day is functionally useless. The donation drive starts before the exchange goes live, not after. Second, Pauline is the primary speaker for the Freelancing Seminar. She is speaking from her own experience as a working student. The seminar carries a clear disclaimer, verbal and on screen, that nothing said constitutes an endorsement of any specific platform or company. This distinction is non-negotiable and it gets communicated in the promotion, not just at the event.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Equipment & Apparel Exchange (Platform) | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| Donation Drive (Pre-Launch) | Dir. EXT/INT | Chief of Operations | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Item Condition Check System | Dir. Student Services | Chief of Staff | Dir. Logistics | Dir. Documentations |\n| Exchange Platform Listing Management | Dir. Student Services | President |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| Freelancing Seminar (Overall) | Dir. Student Services | President | Chief Communications | Dir. Documentations |\n| Seminar Speaker Preparation (Pauline) | President | President | Chief Communications | Dir. Student Services |\n| APS Pre-Act Submission | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Student Services |  |\n| SLIFE Coordination (OCCS, if required) | Dir. EXT/INT | Chief Communications | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Venue Booking (Seminar) | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| Recording Setup (Seminar) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Creatives | Dir. Logistics |\n| Archive Upload to Hub (within 48 hours) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Academics |  |\n| Disclaimer (verbal \\+ on screen) | President | President | Chief Communications | Dir. Creatives |\n| P\\&M for all public-facing items | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. Student Services | Dir. Documentations |\n| All clearance tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff |  |  |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-8 Weeks (Before Term Start)**\n\nDir. EXT/INT launches the pre-launch donation drive. This is targeted at graduating students and those who have recently shifted out of COS programs. The pitch is straightforward: lab gowns, scientific calculators, and tools that served you well should serve someone else next. The drive runs through org communication channels, batch group chats, and coordination with department student organizations to reach the most likely donors. Dir. Documentations submits the SLIFE pre-activity for the donation drive alongside the general outreach plan. 5-8 day lead time.\n\nDir. Student Services drafts the item condition check protocol at the same time. Every donated item goes through a basic assessment before it is listed: tested or untested, functional or not, and condition noted clearly. Every listing carries a visible no-implied-warranty disclaimer. The exchange is donation-based. Students receiving items understand this from the start.\n\nDir. Documentations confirms with DAAM whether the Freelancing Seminar requires a SLIFE form through OCCS in addition to the standard APS. Career-related activities sometimes fall under OCCS jurisdiction. Knowing this at T-8 weeks gives enough time to file both forms at the correct lead times without rushing.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nFirst batch of donated items has come in from the drive. Dir. Student Services runs the condition checks and starts building the initial listing inventory. A platform with ten to fifteen verified, listed items at launch is enough to prove the concept. It does not need to be exhaustive on day one.\n\nDir. Documentations files the APS Pre-Activity form for the Freelancing Seminar. If the OCCS SLIFE is required, it goes in the same week. Both forms should clear well before the target seminar date.\n\nDir. Logistics books the venue for the seminar. Physical venue is preferred over a fully online session since the dynamic between Pauline and the audience is part of what makes this seminar worth attending. A hybrid option can be set up for students who cannot attend in person, but the primary session is live.\n\nDir. Creatives starts developing the P&M materials for both sub-projects. The Equipment Exchange promotion emphasizes the donation ask as much as the recipient benefit. Both sides of the exchange need awareness to function.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nPre-Act approvals should be back by this point. If the SLIFE is still pending, Dir. Documentations flags Chief of Staff immediately.\n\nDir. Creatives submits P&M clearance for the Equipment Exchange platform launch and for the Freelancing Seminar announcement. These can go in together.\n\nRecording setup for the seminar is confirmed. Dir. Documentations owns this specifically. The recording equipment or setup is tested before the event, not on the day of. The 48-hour archive upload window starts the moment the seminar ends, so there is no room for technical issues discovered after the fact.\n\nPauline finalizes the seminar structure with Chief Communications. The session covers freelancing platforms, setting rates, managing client relationships, balancing workload with academic requirements, and legitimate part-time options beyond call center and food service work. The flow is conversational, not a deck-heavy presentation. Pauline speaks from what she has actually done, and that framing is established early in the session alongside the explicit no-endorsement disclaimer.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nEquipment Exchange platform is ready. Listings are live in draft, pending P&M clearance before going public. Dir. Student Services does a final walkthrough of the condition check labels and disclaimer text on each listing.\n\nDonation drive has its final push. Dir. EXT/INT coordinates one last call to graduating students before the exchange goes live.\n\nSeminar promotion goes out once P&M clears. The promotional materials name Pauline as the speaker and frame it accurately: a session on working through college, drawn from real experience. No platform names appear in the promotional materials.\n\n**Week 1-2 of Term: Equipment Exchange Goes Live**\n\nPlatform launches with the initial batch of listed items. The donation channel stays open throughout the term. Dir. Student Services manages incoming donations on a rolling basis, running each item through the condition check before listing. The exchange is not a one-time event. It is a permanent standing feature of the Hub.\n\n**Week 3-4 of Term: Freelancing and Part-Time Work Seminar**\n\nThis window clears GOSM Defense (Jan 19-24) and keeps reasonable distance from Midterms (Feb 16-21). The seminar runs live, hybrid format, with Dir. Documentations managing the recording from start to finish.\n\nAt the opening of the session, Pauline states the disclaimer clearly and on the projected screen behind her: this session reflects personal experience and does not constitute an endorsement of any platform, company, or employer. The disclaimer is not buried in fine print. It is said out loud at the start.\n\nDir. Documentations files the AET feedback form immediately after the session. Feedback forms are collected in person before attendees leave.\n\nWithin 48 hours of the seminar, Dir. Documentations uploads the full recording to the Student Resource Hub on Project 1. This is not optional and it does not extend to 72 hours. The archive is the reason the seminar was recorded. Students who could not attend, and students in future terms, need access to it. Dir. Academics confirms the upload is live on the Hub and accessible.\n\nDir. Documentations files the Post-Activity report within 5 days of the seminar.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\nThe Equipment Exchange is a standing platform feature and runs through all blackout windows without interruption. Donations continue, listings continue, and item checks continue regardless of Midterms, ILW, or the Activity Ban. The Freelancing Seminar targets Week 3-4 of term, well before Midterms. No event scheduling falls in a restricted window.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **Donation drive produces a viable initial listing inventory before the exchange launches:** Dir. EXT/INT runs the drive from T-8 weeks. A minimum threshold of usable, checked, listed items should be in place before P&M is filed for the platform launch. Dir. Student Services and Dir. EXT/INT agree on this threshold number before the drive starts.\n    \n2.  **Item condition check protocol written and in use before the first listing goes live:** Every item passes through the check. Tested or untested is disclosed clearly. No-implied-warranty disclaimer is on every listing page. This is established before launch, not added after a complaint.\n    \n3.  **DAAM confirmation on SLIFE requirement at T-8 weeks:** If the OCCS SLIFE is required and this is discovered at T-4 weeks instead, there may not be enough time to clear it before the target seminar date. Dir. Documentations confirms this early.\n    \n4.  **Recording setup tested before seminar day:** Dir. Documentations owns the recording confirmation. A dry run of the recording setup happens at least two days before the seminar. If there is a technical failure on the actual day, there is no second chance for the 48-hour archive window.\n    \n5.  **Archive upload to Hub confirmed within 48 hours of seminar conclusion:** Dir. Documentations uploads the recording. Dir. Academics confirms it is live and accessible on the Hub. Both confirmations are logged in the clearance tracker. This is a hard deadline, not a target.\n    \n6.  **Disclaimer delivered verbally and on screen at the start of the seminar:** Pauline states it. It is also displayed on the projected screen behind her during the opening. Dir. Creatives prepares the on-screen version as part of the seminar's opening slide. This is not improvised on the day.\n    \n7.  **P&M cleared before any platform or seminar promotion goes public:** Dir. Creatives batches both clearances at T-4 weeks. Neither sub-project is announced before P&M approval is back from DAAM.","subprojects":"# Project 7: Student Financial Empowerment Initiative Sub-Projects\n\n## Equipment & Apparel Exchange\n\n*   **Description:** A donation channel where graduating or shifting students pass down lab gowns, calculators, and tools directly to students who need them.\n\n## Freelancing & Part-Time Work Seminar\n\n*   **Description:** A practical seminar on freelancing and legitimate part time work, so working through college doesn't have to mean a call center shift or a fast food line as the only option."},{"id":"project-8","folderName":"Project_8_Fundraising_Local_Business","name":"Project 8 Fundraising Local Business","faq":"# Fundraising & Local Business Collaboration — FAQ\n\n**Q: Where does the money from these fundraisers actually go?** A: All net proceeds are strictly divided: **70% goes directly to the Student Emergency Fund** (for crisis relief) and **30% goes to the Research Grants Pool** (to offset thesis costs). Every single peso of this split is tracked and publicly visible on the Project 2 Transparency Ledger. It is never mixed into general council funds.\n\n**Q: Why is it taking so long to finalize the partnerships?** A: DAAM requires a minimum 14-day processing lead time to approve External MOAs, and actual processing can run longer depending on corrections or volume. Because the manual does not provide for an umbrella agreement, we process one MOA per business partner. We build our timeline around this lead time from the start — MOA submissions go in at T-8 weeks specifically to absorb delays without jeopardizing the event date. We comply with these rules to make sure the partnerships are legally sound before any student peso is involved.\n\n**Q: What if we don't get any partners and the fair gets cancelled? Does the Emergency Fund run out?** A: No. We have a strict Fallback Plan. If we secure zero partners in a term, we will publicly acknowledge the shortfall on the Transparency Ledger and immediately launch \"Plan B\" — an emergency crowdsourcing drive asking for direct donations from alumni and the student body to ensure the relief funds never hit zero.\n\n**Q: Are the food spots in the Taft Food Crawl sponsored or paid ads?** A: Absolutely not. The Taft Food Crawl operates under a strict 100% non-endorsement policy. We accept zero sponsorship money, paid advertisements, or free meals in exchange for favorable reviews. All spots are crowd-sourced and vetted by student officers and Batch 126 frosh representatives specifically to help students find great food, safe study spaces, and budget-friendly meals.\n\n**Q: Can students recommend their favorite hidden food spots or affordable carinderias to be featured?** A: Yes! Students can submit recommendations through the Suggestion Tab on H2Zero.ai or via our official SCG social media suggestion box. The Academics and External Linkages committees evaluate student submissions, particularly those with meal options priced at ₱50–₱100, for inclusion in upcoming video reels and the downloadable Taft Food Map infographic.","description":"# Pubmat Description\n\nCommunity support means real financial aid. Our Local Business Collaboration Initiative partners with your favorite campus spots to directly fund the Student Emergency Fund (70%) and Research Grants (30%). Support the businesses that support our science scholars. Full transparency, always visible on the SCG Ledger.","executionPlan":"## **Project 8: Fundraising & Local Business Collaboration Initiative**\n\n### **Execution Plan**\n\n**Classification note:** Bucket A for the actual fundraiser events. Each event requires a Special Approval Slip (SCT, 1-3 day lead time) and an External MOA per partner (14-day DAAM lead time). The MOA lead time is the longest single processing requirement in this entire platform. It governs the whole timeline. Every other date in this project moves around it, not the other way around. Note: all financial transactions in this project require specimen signatures on file with OTREAS and the Treasurer's Exam passed by Dir. Finance. See Project 6 pre-launch checklist. Nothing moves without both.\n\nOne thing to establish upfront: this project exists to fund two other projects. The Emergency Micro-Grant Fund and the Research Grants Pool do not have a backup income source if this project underdelivers. That reality should be in the room every time a decision gets made here. Dir. EXT/INT is not running a nice-to-have community fair. They are running the financial backbone of the welfare platform.\n\n* * *\n\n### **RACI Matrix**\n\n| Area | Responsible | Accountable | Consulted | Informed |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Partner Identification & Outreach | Dir. EXT/INT | President | Chief Communications | Dir. Documentations |\n| MOA Negotiation (per partner) | Dir. EXT/INT | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| MOA DAAM Submission (per partner) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. EXT/INT | President |\n| MOA 14-Day Lead Time Tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff |  | Dir. EXT/INT |\n| SCT Submission per Event | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Operations | Dir. Finance |  |\n| Event Logistics (booth setup, venue) | Dir. Logistics | Chief of Operations | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Documentations |\n| Proceeds Collection and Deposit | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. EXT/INT | President |\n| DF Proceeds Split (70/30) | Dir. Finance | President | Chief of Staff | Dir. Documentations |\n| FRA Report Filing (per event) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations | Dir. Documentations | President |\n| Transparency Ledger Update (post-FAO) | Dir. Finance | Chief of Operations |  | Dir. Documentations |\n| Plan B Crowdfunding Drive | Chief Communications | President | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Finance |\n| Taft Food Guide Merchant Curation & Vetting | Dir. EXT/INT | President | Batch 126 Reps | Dir. Creatives |\n| Taft Food Crawl Video Production & Skits | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Batch 126 Reps, Pauline | Dir. Documentations |\n| Taft Food Map Infographic Design | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Documentations |\n| Food Guide P&M Clearances (Videos & Infographic) | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Creatives | Batch 126 Government |\n| P\\&M for all public-facing promotion | Dir. Creatives | Chief Communications | Dir. EXT/INT | Dir. Documentations |\n| All clearance and submission tracking | Dir. Documentations | Chief of Staff | Dir. Finance |  |\n\n* * *\n\n### **Proposed Timeline**\n\n**T-12 Weeks (Before Target Event Date)**\n\nPartner identification starts here, and the target is three to five confirmed partners per event. Dir. EXT/INT draws up a shortlist of local businesses with a natural connection to the COS student base: food businesses run by students or alumni, tutoring services, printing shops, lab supply vendors, and similar. The pitch to each partner is clear and honest: a fixed percentage of proceeds from a designated window goes to student emergency relief and research grants. The amount raised is tracked publicly on the Transparency Ledger.\n\nThree to five partners is the minimum, not the ceiling. Going after more than that from the start means that if two or three decline or drop out, the event still runs. Dir. EXT/INT and Chief Communications develop the partner pitch together. Chief Communications handles any formal outreach letters. Dir. EXT/INT handles the direct conversations.\n\nAt the same time, Dir. Finance reviews the DF ring-fence floor declared in Project 6. The projected income from this project needs to be mapped against that floor so Dir. Finance knows exactly how much the fundraising events need to generate for the micro-grant fund to have anything left to disburse after the carry-over minimum is protected.\n\n**T-10 Weeks**\n\nPartner conversations are underway. Dir. EXT/INT is in active negotiation with each shortlisted business. The target is to have three partners verbally committed by this point so MOA drafting can start.\n\nEach partner gets its own MOA. The Treasurer's Manual does not provide for a single umbrella MOA covering multiple vendors, so each agreement is drafted and processed individually. Dir. EXT/INT and Chief of Staff draft the MOA template together. The core terms are identical across partners: the event dates, the proceeds split percentage the partner is committing, how proceeds are collected and deposited, and the acknowledgment that the SCG is a student organization operating under DLSU governance. Individual partner-specific terms go in as addenda.\n\n**T-8 Weeks**\n\nThis is the hard gate. All External MOAs must be submitted to DAAM by this point at the absolute latest. The 14-day processing lead time means a submission at T-8 weeks clears by T-6 weeks, which gives enough buffer before the event to handle any resubmissions if DAAM sends something back with corrections.\n\nDir. Documentations submits every MOA as soon as it is finalized. Not in batches, not waiting for all partners to be ready at once. Each MOA goes in the moment the draft is approved by Pauline and the partner. The 14-day clock starts from individual submission, so staggering is fine.\n\nDir. EXT/INT also ensures that all External MOA submissions land by January 16 at the latest, which respects the DAAM External MOA lead time relative to the first event window. Dir. Documentations tracks each submission individually with a status column per partner in the clearance tracker.\n\n**T-6 Weeks**\n\nMOAs should be clearing from DAAM at this point. For any that come back with corrections, Dir. Documentations and Dir. EXT/INT turn them around same day and resubmit immediately.\n\nDir. Documentations files the SCT for each fundraiser event (1-3 day lead time). The SCT is filed per event, not per partner. If two events are planned, two SCTs go in.\n\nDir. Logistics begins venue coordination for the physical fair setup. Booth assignments, power access, table arrangements, and any college-level approvals for use of outdoor or common spaces go through Dir. Logistics in coordination with the Dean's Office if required.\n\nDir. Creatives starts developing promotional materials for the fair. The promotion strategy serves a dual purpose: it drives student foot traffic to the event and signals to partner businesses that the SCG is actively promoting their participation, which protects the relationship if partners question whether the collaboration is worth their time.\n\n**T-4 Weeks**\n\nAll MOAs are cleared. All SCTs confirmed. Dir. Finance confirms the projected income estimate based on confirmed partners and their committed proceeds percentages.\n\nDir. Creatives submits P&M clearance for all promotional materials. Promotion goes live once P&M clears. Dir. EXT/INT coordinates with each partner on what they are allowed to say in their own channels about the collaboration, making sure none of their promotion inadvertently bypasses the DAAM clearance requirements on the SCG side.\n\n**T-2 Weeks**\n\nFull promotional push. Dir. EXT/INT confirms final event logistics with each partner: booth location, setup time, payment collection method, and how proceeds are handed over to the SCG. All payments route through the dedicated SCG account. No personal accounts, no exception.\n\nDir. Finance prepares the income tracking sheet for the event. Every deposit is logged with the partner name, date, and amount. This feeds directly into the FRA Report after the event.\n\n**Event Week (Target: Late February, Post-Midterms)**\n\nThe Local Business Fair runs. Dir. Logistics manages physical setup. Dir. EXT/INT manages partner relations on the ground. Dir. Finance manages proceeds collection and ensures all payments are deposited into the SCG account with deposit slips filed.\n\nProceeds collection is clean and documented from the first transaction. Every peso that comes in goes through the account, not through anyone's hands. Dir. Finance brings a physical receipt book to the event if needed.\n\n**Post-Event: FRA Report and Proceeds Transfer**\n\nFAO processes all receipts first. The Transparency Ledger update goes public only after FAO confirmation. The feasibility analysis is explicit on this sequence and it is the right one. A public ledger update before FAO confirmation is a promise made on unconfirmed numbers, which is exactly the kind of thing that creates credibility problems.\n\nOnce FAO confirms all receipts, Dir. Finance files the full FRA Report within one week of the event's conclusion. The report includes: the Fundraising Activity Report, Income Statement, approved deposit slip from OTREAS, screenshot of the OTREAS email acknowledgment, compiled confirmation receipts in one PDF, and the approved A-Form with Proof of Approval.\n\nDir. Finance then formally transfers proceeds into the correct buckets: 70% into the DF Emergency Micro-Grant ring-fenced allocation, 30% into the Research Grants Pool. Both transfers are reflected on the Transparency Ledger with the date of FAO confirmation noted clearly.\n\n**Plan B: If Zero Partners Are Secured**\n\nThis is not a theoretical contingency. It is a pre-planned operational response. If Dir. EXT/INT reaches T-8 weeks without a single confirmed partner, Chief Communications activates the Emergency Crowdfunding Drive immediately. The drive is announced publicly with full transparency about why: the local business partnership model did not come together this term and the SCG is pursuing direct community support instead. The 70/30 proceeds split applies to crowdfunding income using the same logic as the fair.\n\nThe Plan B activation is not a failure to communicate externally. It is the council being honest about what happened and moving quickly to protect the funds that Project 6 depends on.\n\n**Blackout Compliance**\n\nThe target event window is late February, immediately after Midterms end (Feb 21). This gives the maximum available term window after the GOSM Defense period and Midterms, while still landing before ILW (Mar 2-7) and the Activity Ban (Mar 30). MOA submissions at T-8 weeks fall in early January, well before any blackout restrictions. P&M clearance at T-4 weeks falls in early February, also clear. No event activity falls inside a restricted window.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Taft Food Crawl & Local Business Guide: Operational Rollout (Term 1)**\n\n**Classification Note:** Media Coverage / Awareness Campaign (Bucket B / P&M Clearance with Joint Batch Collaboration). Carried out in formal partnership with FOCUS2026 (Batch 126 Government). Requires 24-hour P&M clearance per episode drop and master infographic release. Zero council budget allocated (₱0.00).\n\n- **T-4 Weeks (October 7 – 14, 2026):**\n  - Joint coordination meeting between SCG Executive Board (Pauline, Dir. EXT/INT, Dir. Creatives) and Batch 126 Representatives.\n  - Establish merchant shortlist across 5 categories: Chicken spots, Vietnamese/Asian bowls, Cheap scholar carinderias (₱50–₱100), Coffee/Study spots, and Desserts.\n  - Vetting criteria enforced: Cleanliness, student-budget friendliness, walking safety, and strict non-endorsement policy (no paid ads or sponsored favoritism).\n\n- **T-2 Weeks (October 21 – 28, 2026):**\n  - Scripting and on-location filming of 4 vlog-style video reels/TikToks featuring Pauline and Batch 126 representatives.\n  - Dir. Creatives compiles data, price ranges, walking routes, and scholar hacks for the master Taft Food Map Infographic.\n  - Dir. Documentations submits batch P&M clearances for promotional teasers, video reels, and the infographic map.\n\n- **Episode Drop Schedule (Term 1):**\n  - **November 4, 2026 (Episode 1):** *The Taft Chicken Run* — Comparing fast-casual chicken joints, portion sizes, and budget options (24 Chicken, Bok, Uncle John's).\n  - **November 11, 2026 (Episode 2):** *Vietnamese & Asian Comfort Bowls* — Exploring banh mi, pho, and noodle spots for rainy-day study sessions.\n  - **November 18, 2026 (Episode 3):** *The Scholar Survival Guide: ₱50–₱100 Carinderia Secrets* — Spotlighting Agno Food Court, Bloemen Hall, Castro, and Leon Guinto gems so scholars can invite peers out without financial anxiety.\n  - **November 25, 2026 (Episode 4 & Map Release):** *Late-Night Coffee, Study Havens & Master Taft Food Map Infographic Release* — Outlets, study atmospheres, plus the release of the high-res downloadable PDF/PNG food map pinned on H2Zero.ai and SCG socials.\n\n* * *\n\n### **Dependencies**\n\n1.  **Three confirmed partners minimum before any MOA is drafted:** Dir. EXT/INT and Pauline agree on a partner floor before outreach begins. Going into MOA drafting with one confirmed partner and hoping for more is not a strategy. The floor is three. If only two partners confirm by T-10 weeks, outreach expands before MOA drafting starts.\n    \n2.  **All External MOAs submitted to DAAM by T-8 weeks, no later than January 16:** The 14-day processing requirement makes this the most time-sensitive filing in the entire platform. Dir. Documentations tracks each MOA individually from the moment drafting begins. A single MOA submitted late can delay an entire partner's participation.\n    \n3.  **All proceeds routed through the dedicated SCG account, no exceptions:** Dir. Finance establishes this as a standing rule before the first partner conversation happens. It goes into every MOA as a stated term. Partners know before they sign that payment collection follows the SCG's formal financial process.\n    \n4.  **FRA Report filed within one week of event conclusion, after FAO confirmation:** Dir. Finance leads this with Dir. Documentations compiling the receipt PDF. If net proceeds exceed ₱5,000, the full FRA Report is required. Given the fundraising targets, it almost certainly will be. The report is prepared in advance with all fields ready, so only the confirmed figures need to be filled in after FAO confirmation.\n    \n5.  **Transparency Ledger update goes out after FAO confirmation, not before:** Dir. Finance and Dir. Documentations both understand this sequencing. The 48-hour public communication standard from Project 3 applies here, but the clock starts from FAO confirmation, not from the close of the event.\n    \n6.  **70/30 split transferred formally into the correct allocations before any disbursement is made from either pool:** Dir. Finance completes the transfer and logs it on the Transparency Ledger before Project 6 or the Research Grants Portal draws from the new income. The split is not approximate. It is exact, documented, and public.\n    \n7.  **Plan B Crowdfunding Drive brief prepared in advance:** Chief Communications drafts the Plan B announcement before it is needed. If the trigger condition is reached, the response goes out within 24 hours, not after a week of internal deliberation. The draft exists, the channels are identified, and the activation decision belongs to Pauline alone.\n\n8.  **Strict Non-Endorsement & Scholar Budget Vetting for Taft Food Guide:** Dir. EXT/INT and Batch 126 Reps ensure all reviewed eateries are selected organically by students based on affordability and utility. Zero sponsored placements or commercial endorsement fees are accepted; all cheap scholar picks (₱50–₱100) must be independently price-verified before filming.","subprojects":"# Project 8: Fundraising & Local Business Collaboration Initiative Sub-Projects\n\n## Fundraiser Events & Local Business Partnerships\n\n*   **Description:** A recurring partnership program with local businesses near campus. A percentage of proceeds from a promotion period goes to the Emergency Fund (70%) and Research Grants (30%).\n*   **Timeline:** Event/Partnerships. Requires Special Approval Slip (SCT) 1-3 days + External MOA 14 days per partner.\n*   **Feasibility:** Feasible if aiming for 3-5 small partners. Fallback plan: Emergency Crowdfunding Drive if 0 partners are secured.\n\n## Taft Food Crawl: COS Student Food & Local Business Guide (with Batch 126)\n\n*   **Description:** A collaborative digital lifestyle series, culinary survival guide, and local merchant showcase co-produced by the Science College Government (SCG) and newly inducted Batch 126 (frosh) representatives. The project explores, reviews, and maps food establishments around campus across multiple categories, highlighting cheap, high-nutrition alternatives (₱50–₱100) so science scholars can comfortably invite friends and dine out without budget anxiety.\n*   **Content Categories:**\n    *   **Chicken Spots:** Fast-casual, fried chicken joints, and tenders around Taft (e.g., 24 Chicken, Bok, Uncle John's).\n    *   **Vietnamese & Asian Rice Bowls:** Affordable pho, banh mi, noodles, and rice meal spots (e.g., Banh Mi Kitchen, Agno Asian bowls).\n    *   **Cheap Scholar Alternatives & Secret Carinderias:** Hidden budget gems, student value carinderias, and canteen meals (Agno Food Court, Bloemen Hall, Castro St., Leon Guinto) priced between ₱50 and ₱100.\n    *   **Coffee Havens & Study Cafes:** Late-night study spots with power outlets, reliable Wi-Fi, and budget-friendly caffeine fixes.\n    *   **Desserts, Shakes & Quick Bites:** Quick snacks, fruit shakes, and sweet treats between laboratory classes.\n*   **Deliverables:**\n    *   **4 Episodic Vlog-Style Video Reels / TikToks:** Fun, relatable skits and taste reviews co-hosted by Pauline and Batch 126 representatives showing frosh how to navigate Taft food options.\n    *   **1 Master Taft Food Map Infographic:** A high-resolution, downloadable digital food map and cheat sheet categorizing price ranges, walking distances, and scholar picks, pinned on the SCG portal and social channels.\n*   **Timeline & Rollout:** Term Long (Weekly episodic releases starting November 4, 11, 18, and 25, 2026).\n*   **Clearance & Governance:** Joint Student Service / Media Coverage initiative with FOCUS2026 (Batch 126 Government). Requires P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) for video reels and infographic releases; 100% organic student curation with strict non-endorsement policy (zero paid commercial promotions).\n*   **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds (filmed using student smartphones; zero production overhead)."}],"preActs":{"project-2":{"name":"Project 2 Semana ng Siyensya","files":[{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts (Internal)\n\n## 1. Classification\n- **Type**: Major College Event (On-Ground & Online)\n- **Primary Beneficiary**: Food Security Pantry & Emergency Micro-Grant\n- **Budget Requisition**: ₱15,000 (Merchandise Downpayment, Logistics)\n\n## 2. Required Clearances\n- [ ] **SLIFE Approval**: Pending submission 1 month prior.\n- [ ] **SDA Clearance**: Required for E-Sports event (Games and Amusement regulations).\n- [ ] **Dean's Office Endorsement**: Required for Academic Incentives integration.\n- [ ] **GOSM Registration**: Completed.\n- [ ] **P&M Clearance**: Pending material designs.\n"}]},"project-3":{"name":"Project 3 Transparency Platform","files":[{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Transparency & Accountability Platform\n**Project 3 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nUnder the **DLSU USG DAAM Operations Manual**, all digital features of Project 3 (Website, Centralized Grievance Channel, Financial Transparency Ledger, and Student Rights Charter) are classified as **Non-Synchronous Online Platforms / Standing Systems**.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Universal Pre-Act Mandate:**  \n> Contrary to the common misconception that online platforms do not need pre-acts, DAAM strictly mandates that this platform must be formally processed and approved via a **General Pre-Activity (thru APS; Others)** form prior to public rollout.\n> \n> Once approved, DAAM issues an **Activity Reference Code (ARC)**. All individual feature announcements, infographics, and rights campaigns are then processed via **Publicity Materials (P&M) with a 24-hour lead time** using this ARC code.\n\n---\n\n### Documentation Requirements for Platform Launch\n1. **Activity Form (A-Form):** Marked *\"Online Activity: YES\"*, *\"Activity in GOSM: YES\"*, *\"Activity Type: thru APS; Others\"*, with `Others: Digital Governance & Transparency Website` (GOSM Row 35), `Others: Encrypted Online Student Grievance Channel` (GOSM Row 34), and `Others: Digital Student Rights Handbook & Advocacy Campaign` (GOSM Row 61).\n2. **Comprehensive Project Description (CPD):** **Table 1 Only** (per DAAM Manual Page 119 for non-synchronous online platforms). Table 2 program flow is omitted.\n3. **Breakdown of Expenses Table:** Retained in full with **\"N/A\"** declared (₱0.00 council funds; zero commercial hosting costs via open-source client-side architecture).\n4. **Key Pre-Act Attachments:**\n   * **Centralized Grievance Channel:** Written confirmation letter of external faculty ombudsperson / mediator.\n   * **Transparency Ledger:** Written 72-hour posting protocol for all council disbursements $> ₱500.00$.\n   * **Student Rights Charter:** Verification summary against DLSU Student Handbook provisions.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every Pre-Act submission, ARC code issuance, and P&M clearance here to maintain a strict paper trail.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | Pre-Act Submission (APS: Others) | Pending | Lead time: 4–6 working days before term launch |\n| | External Mediator Confirmation | Pending | Hard dependency for Grievance Channel |\n| | P&M Submission (Batch 1: Launch) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n"}]},"project-4":{"name":"Project 4 Inclusion Accessibility","files":[{"filename":"FSL_Training_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Filipino Sign Language (FSL) Training Program\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Filipino Sign Language (FSL) Training Program\n- **Nature of Activity:** Community Development\n- **Type of Activity:** Seminar / Workshop\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 10 / 50\n- **Date:** February 14, 2027\n- **Time:** 1:00 PM - 5:00 PM\n- **Venue:** Gokongwei Hall Room 201 (or equivalent)\n- **Project Head(s):** Director for External and Internal Linkages\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* The Deaf and hard-of-hearing community remains an afterthought in our classrooms and laboratories. Often, there is a distinct lack of communicators within the student body capable of basic conversational FSL, leading to systemic exclusion in collaborative academic spaces.\n*(Part 2)* This exclusion exists because FSL is rarely integrated into standard curriculum or extracurricular training. We feel that science must be accessible to everyone, and communication barriers directly hinder the academic experience of marginalized students. \n*(Part 3)* By partnering with a recognized Deaf community organization, we will equip student leaders and volunteers with foundational FSL skills. This ensures that our college takes a concrete step toward becoming a truly inclusive environment.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To train 50 student leaders and volunteers in basic conversational Filipino Sign Language.\n2. To establish a long-term partnership with a recognized Deaf community organization.\n3. To foster an inclusive laboratory and classroom environment by reducing communication barriers.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM DESIGN**\n- 1:00 PM - 1:30 PM: Registration\n- 1:30 PM - 2:00 PM: Introduction to Deaf Culture & Etiquette\n- 2:00 PM - 4:00 PM: Basic FSL Instruction (Alphabet, Greetings, Academic terms)\n- 4:00 PM - 4:30 PM: Interactive Practice Session\n- 4:30 PM - 4:55 PM: Closing & AET Evaluation\n- 4:55 PM - 5:00 PM: Clean-up\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Honorarium for Partner Organization Instructors: Php 5,000.00\n- **Total:** Php 5,000.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Organizational Funds: Php 5,000.00\n- **Total:** Php 5,000.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n*THIS ACTIVITY IS NOT A FUNDRAISING/SELLING ACTIVITY; THUS, IT WILL NOT INCUR INCOME OR LOSS.*\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Accumulated Operational Funds: N/A\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 5,000.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 5,000.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. PROVISIONS FOR PROFIT AND LOSS**\nThe Chief Operating Officer and Chairperson for Finance shall be held liable and will shoulder any remaining balance incurred by the project.\n"},{"filename":"Lab_Audit_Letter.md","content":"**Date:** January 27, 2027\n\n**To:** \nDr. [Insert Name]\nDean, College of Science\n\n[Insert Name]\nDirector, Physical Facilities Management\n\n**Thru:** \nMr. James B. Laxa\nDirector, Office of Student LIFE\n\n**From:** \n[Insert Name]\nDirector for Advocacy, College of Science Government\n\n**Re:** Request for Formal Access for Laboratory Accessibility Audit\n\nGreetings in St. La Salle!\n\nThe College of Science Government (SCG) is initiating a comprehensive Laboratory Accessibility Audit to identify and document physical barriers that hinder students with disabilities from fully participating in laboratory classes. This initiative aligns with the University's commitment to inclusive education.\n\nIn line with this, we formally request access to conduct a student-led physical walkthrough and audit of all College of Science laboratory facilities from **March 9 to March 13, 2027**. \n\nUpon completion of the audit, we will submit a formal report detailing our findings and recommended fixes. **We respectfully request a formal response and/or remediation plan from your respective offices within ninety (90) days of the report's submission (Deadline: May 2, 2027).** Please be advised that in the interest of transparency and student welfare, if no substantive response or remediation plan is provided by this 90-day deadline, the council will publish the full audit report publicly on our Transparency Platform.\n\nShould you have any questions or clarifications, you may contact me at [Email Address] & [Mobile Number].\n\nWe look forward to your favorable response. Thank you very much!\n\nSincerely,\n\n[Insert Name]\nDirector for Advocacy, SCG\n\nNoted By:\n\n[Insert Name]\nCollege President, SCG\n"},{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation\n**Project 4 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nThis project encompasses both physical accessibility initiatives and standing digital representation repositories:\n\n1. **Women and Minorities in STEM Archive (Term 1, Yearlong — GOSM Row 36):**\n   * **Classification:** Non-Synchronous Online Platform / Digital Repository.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **General Pre-Activity (`thru APS; Others`)** with `Others: Digital Representation & Research Archive`.\n   * **Lead Time:** 4–6 working days prior to public launch.\n   * **Requirements:** A-Form, CPD Table 1 only, N/A Expense Table, and mandatory signed **RA 10173 Opt-In Consent Form templates**.\n   * **Publicity:** P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) using the issued ARC code prior to rolling out individual profile cards.\n\n2. **Filipino Sign Language (FSL) Training Program (Term 2):**\n   * **Classification:** Synchronous Training Program / External Tie-Up.\n   * **DAAM Route:** General Pre-Activity (APS: 4–6 days) and SLIFE (5–8 days for cross-organizational tie-up).\n   * **Requirements:** Full CPD Table 1 & Table 2, and formal **External Memorandum of Agreement (External MOA: 14-day minimum lead time via MST)** with Deaf community partners.\n\n3. **Laboratory Accessibility Audit (Term 2):**\n   * **Classification:** Administrative Walkthrough & Physical Facility Assessment.\n   * **DAAM Route:** Letters and Requests (2–3 working days) addressed to the College Dean and Physical Facilities Management (PFM) establishing a 90-day administrative remediation deadline.\n\n---\n\n### Included Documents\n- `FSL_Training_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the FSL Training Program.\n- `Lab_Audit_Letter.md` — Formal request letter to the Dean's Office and PFM establishing the 90-day deadline.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every Pre-Act submission, MOA status, and P&M approval here to maintain a strict paper trail.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | STEM Archive Pre-Act (APS: Others) | Pending | Lead time: 4–6 working days (CPD Table 1 + N/A Expense) |\n| | STEM Archive RA 10173 Consent Forms | Pending | Required prior to public profile publishing |\n| | P&M Submission (STEM Profiles) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n"}]},"project-5":{"name":"Project 5 Queer Trans Education","files":[{"filename":"PreColonial_History_Session_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session\n- **Nature of Activity:** Awareness Campaign\n- **Type of Activity:** Seminar / Workshop\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 10 / 100\n- **Date:** March 11, 2027\n- **Time:** 2:00 PM - 5:00 PM\n- **Venue:** Yuchangco Hall (or equivalent)\n- **Project Head(s):** Director for National Affairs\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* There is a persistent misconception that queer and trans identities are a modern, Western import. This erasure of pre-colonial Philippine history contributes to the marginalization of LGBTQ+ Filipinos today.\n*(Part 2)* Without understanding the historical reverence for gender diversity in our own culture—such as the role of the Babaylan and Asog—students are deprived of the cultural context that proves queer identities are inherently Filipino.\n*(Part 3)* This session will explore gender diversity in pre-colonial Philippine history, providing students with the historical backing to understand that the conversation around queer rights is not new, but rather a returning one.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To educate 100 students on the history of gender diversity in the pre-colonial Philippines.\n2. To dispel the myth that queer and trans identities are a Western influence.\n3. To contextualize modern LGBTQ+ rights movements (e.g., the SOGIE bill) within Philippine historical precedents.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM DESIGN**\n- 2:00 PM - 2:30 PM: Registration\n- 2:30 PM - 3:30 PM: Main Lecture: The Babaylan, Asog, and Pre-Colonial Gender Diversity\n- 3:30 PM - 4:15 PM: Moderated Panel Discussion\n- 4:15 PM - 4:45 PM: Open Forum / Q&A\n- 4:45 PM - 4:55 PM: Closing & AET Evaluation\n- 4:55 PM - 5:00 PM: Clean-up\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Speaker Tokens (Certificates & University Merchandise): Php 1,500.00\n- **Total:** Php 1,500.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Organizational Funds: Php 1,500.00\n- **Total:** Php 1,500.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n*THIS ACTIVITY IS NOT A FUNDRAISING/SELLING ACTIVITY; THUS, IT WILL NOT INCUR INCOME OR LOSS.*\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Accumulated Operational Funds: N/A\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 1,500.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 1,500.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. PROVISIONS FOR PROFIT AND LOSS**\nThe Chief Operating Officer and Chairperson for Finance shall be held liable and will shoulder any remaining balance incurred by the project.\n"},{"filename":"Queer_Identity_Forum_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Queer Identity Science Forum\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Queer Identity Science Forum\n- **Nature of Activity:** Awareness Campaign\n- **Type of Activity:** Seminar / Workshop with Distinguished Speakers\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 10 / 100\n- **Date:** February 11, 2027\n- **Time:** 2:00 PM - 5:00 PM\n- **Venue:** Yuchangco Hall (or equivalent)\n- **Project Head(s):** Director for Advocacy\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* Queer and trans identities are frequently invalidated by claims that they are \"unnatural\" or \"unscientific.\" In a College of Science, allowing prejudice to masquerade as objective fact is unacceptable.\n*(Part 2)* The proliferation of these myths stems from a lack of accessible, rigorous education on the biology, sociology, and psychology of gender and sexuality. Students need a space where their identities are validated by actual science, not debated as opinions.\n*(Part 3)* By inviting credentialed academics to present peer-reviewed research, this forum will counter anti-LGBTQ+ prejudice with undeniable academic evidence, fostering a safer, more informed campus culture.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To educate 100 students on the biological, psychological, and sociological realities of queer and trans identities.\n2. To provide a platform for credentialed academics to share peer-reviewed research countering gender essentialism.\n3. To foster an unapologetically affirming academic environment for LGBTQ+ students in the College of Science.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM DESIGN**\n- 2:00 PM - 2:30 PM: Registration\n- 2:30 PM - 3:15 PM: Speaker 1 (The Biology of Sex & Gender)\n- 3:15 PM - 4:00 PM: Speaker 2 (The Sociology of Queer Identities)\n- 4:00 PM - 4:45 PM: Open Forum / Q&A\n- 4:45 PM - 4:55 PM: Closing & AET Evaluation\n- 4:55 PM - 5:00 PM: Clean-up\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Speaker Tokens (Certificates & University Merchandise): Php 1,500.00\n- **Total:** Php 1,500.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Organizational Funds: Php 1,500.00\n- **Total:** Php 1,500.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n*THIS ACTIVITY IS NOT A FUNDRAISING/SELLING ACTIVITY; THUS, IT WILL NOT INCUR INCOME OR LOSS.*\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Accumulated Operational Funds: N/A\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 1,500.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 1,500.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. PROVISIONS FOR PROFIT AND LOSS**\nThe Chief Operating Officer and Chairperson for Finance shall be held liable and will shoulder any remaining balance incurred by the project.\n"},{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Queer & Trans Identity Education Series\n**Project 5 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nThis initiative combines a permanent educational platform launch with expert-led academic seminars:\n\n1. **Shanghay Laya: Malaya Maging Ikaw (Platform Launch: Nov 7, 2026 — GOSM Row 116):**\n   * **Classification:** Non-Synchronous Online Educational Repository.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **General Pre-Activity (`thru APS; Others`)** with `Others: Digital Educational Platform Launch`.\n   * **Lead Time:** 4–6 working days prior to public launch.\n   * **Requirements:** A-Form, CPD Table 1 only, N/A Expense Table, and a **Formal Legal Disclaimer Sign-Off Sheet** confirming provider licensing (endocrinologists, affirming clinics).\n   * **Publicity:** P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) using the issued ARC code prior to social media publication.\n\n2. **Queer Identity Science Forum (Term 2 / Post-Midterms):**\n   * **Classification:** Academic Seminar with Distinguished External Speakers.\n   * **DAAM Route:** General Pre-Activity (APS: 4–6 days) or SLIFE Seminar with Distinguished Speakers (5–8 days).\n   * **Requirements:** Full CPD Table 1 & Table 2 (with AET 5m before end), verified speaker peer-reviewed publication list, and room reservation ticket.\n\n3. **Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session (Term 2 / Post-Midterms):**\n   * **Classification:** Historical & Cultural Education Masterclass.\n   * **DAAM Route:** General Pre-Activity (APS: 4–6 days) or SLIFE (5–8 days).\n   * **Requirements:** Full CPD Table 1 & Table 2, academic credential verification, and room reservation ticket.\n\n---\n\n### Included Documents\n- `Queer_Identity_Forum_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the Science Forum.\n- `PreColonial_History_Session_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the History Session.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every Pre-Act submission, disclaimer sign-off, and P&M approval here to maintain a strict paper trail.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | Shanghay Laya Pre-Act (APS: Others) | Pending | Lead time: 4–6 working days (Target Launch: Nov 7, 2026) |\n| | Legal Disclaimer Sign-Off Sheet | Pending | Hard gate: provider directory review |\n| | P&M Submission (Shanghay Laya Launch) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n"}]},"project-6":{"name":"Project 6 Emergency Response","files":[{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Student Emergency Response & Relief Program\n**Project 6 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nProject 6 directly manages student disaster relief, emergency food security, and student welfare support. Every component is subject to strict USG DAAM and OTREAS compliance:\n\n1. **Sakuna Relief: Emergency Response & Food Security Pantry (Term Long — GOSM Row 62):**\n   * **Classification:** Student Welfare Relief & Food Security Initiative.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **SLIFE Activity (`thru SLIFE; Others`)** with `Others: Emergency Disaster Relief & Welfare Pantry`.\n   * **Lead Time:** **5–8 working days**.\n   * **Financial Allocation:** **₱3,000.00 Operational Fund (OF)** approved under `T1 LOE` Row 12, itemized under:\n     * GL 512201001 (Food and Meals): ₱2,000.00 (Emergency canned proteins, easy-open meals, drinking water).\n     * GL 511001004 (Other Materials and Supplies): ₱1,000.00 (Sanitary hygiene supplies, packaging/shelving).\n   * **Required Documentation:**\n     1. A-Form endorsed to SLIFE Coordinator (Mr. Zaldy Dueñas III) and Director SLIFE.\n     2. Comprehensive Project Description (CPD Table 1).\n     3. Project Requisition Slip (PRS) & Direct Payment (Procurement) Memo.\n     4. Venue/Exhibit Area Approval (St. Joseph Hall 1st Floor Front Lobby / Velasco Lobby per RRS Sec 6.5/6.11).\n   * **Post-Activity (ADM):** Liquidation report and inventory distribution log filed within 5 working days post-distribution.\n\n2. **Welfare Outreach Registry (Term Long, Online Intake):**\n   * **Classification:** Confidential Disaster Vulnerability Database.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **SLIFE Activity (`thru SLIFE; Others`)** for student intake.\n   * **Compliance Standards:** Strict adherence to RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act of 2012). Private tier outreach during calm weather; mandatory termly data wipe; zero public disclosure of recipient names.\n\n3. **Emergency Micro-Grant Relief Fund (Depository Fund Allocation):**\n   * **Classification:** Student Financial Aid & Calamity Support.\n   * **Funding Source:** 100% Depository Fund (DF) ring-fenced balance, replenished via Project 8 proceeds. ₱0.00 Operational Fund.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every SLIFE submission, PRS status, and inventory restocking record here.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | Sakuna Relief Pre-Act (`thru SLIFE; Others`) | Pending | Lead time: 5–8 working days (₱3,000.00 OF) |\n| | St. Joseph Lobby Exhibit Area Booking | Pending | 100% Free under RRS Sec 6.5/6.11 |\n| | P&M Submission (Pantry Operations) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n| | Post-Activity Liquidation & ADM | Pending | Due 5 working days post-distribution |\n"}]},"project-7":{"name":"Project 7 Financial Empowerment","files":[{"filename":"Freelancing_Seminar_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Freelancing & Part-Time Work Seminar\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Freelancing & Part-Time Work Seminar\n- **Nature of Activity:** Student Welfare / Skill-Building\n- **Type of Activity:** Seminar / Workshop\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 10 / 150\n- **Date:** February 12, 2027\n- **Time:** 2:00 PM - 5:00 PM\n- **Venue:** Andrew Gonzalez Hall (or equivalent large venue)\n- **Project Head(s):** Director for Student Services\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* The cost of a science degree extends far beyond tuition—lab fees, manuals, and specialized equipment create a heavy burden. An increasing number of students are turning to part-time work and freelancing to fund their own education.\n*(Part 2)* However, many students lack the foundational knowledge to navigate the freelance market, risking exploitation, burnout, or academic failure.\n*(Part 3)* This seminar will provide practical, lived-experience insights on balancing academics with part-time work, securing clients, and managing time effectively, equipping self-funding students with the tools they need to survive and thrive.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To equip 150 students with practical strategies for securing and managing part-time freelance work.\n2. To provide a realistic framework for balancing heavy academic science workloads with employment.\n3. To generate a permanent video archive of the seminar for the Student Resource Hub.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM DESIGN**\n- 2:00 PM - 2:30 PM: Registration\n- 2:30 PM - 2:40 PM: Opening Remarks & Disclaimer (Lived Experience, No Endorsements)\n- 2:40 PM - 3:40 PM: Main Lecture: Surviving Science as a Working Student (Speaker: Pauline)\n- 3:40 PM - 4:20 PM: Open Forum / Q&A\n- 4:20 PM - 4:45 PM: Recording Sign-off & AET Evaluation\n- 4:45 PM - 5:00 PM: Clean-up\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Venue Technical Fees (Microphones/Projector): Php 0.00 (Standard University Provision)\n- Speaker Tokens: Php 0.00 (Internal SCG Speaker)\n- **Total:** Php 0.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Organizational Funds: Php 0.00\n- **Total:** Php 0.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n*THIS ACTIVITY IS NOT A FUNDRAISING/SELLING ACTIVITY; THUS, IT WILL NOT INCUR INCOME OR LOSS.*\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Accumulated Operational Funds: N/A\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 0.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 0.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. PROVISIONS FOR PROFIT AND LOSS**\nThe Chief Operating Officer and Chairperson for Finance shall be held liable and will shoulder any remaining balance incurred by the project.\n"},{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Student Financial Empowerment Initiative\n**Project 7 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nProject 7 removes systemic financial hurdles for College of Science students through physical resource circularity and peer-led career development:\n\n1. **Equipment & Apparel Exchange: Lab Gown & Calculator Drive (Term Long — GOSM Row 63):**\n   * **Classification:** Physical Donation Drive & Permanent Circular Resource Pool.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **`Donation Drive` (processed through SLIFE)**.\n   * **Lead Time:** **5–8 working days**.\n   * **Physical Venues:** St. Joseph Hall 1st Floor Lobby & Br. Andrew Gonzalez Hall Ground Floor drop-off bins (100% Free under RRS Sec 6.5/6.11).\n   * **Required Documentation:**\n     1. A-Form submitted to SLIFE.\n     2. CPD Table 1 detailing donation handling and sanitation protocols.\n     3. Item Condition Assessment Protocol (testing, grading, and \"No Implied Warranty\" disclaimer).\n     4. N/A Breakdown of Expenses Table (₱0.00 council funds; 100% peer donation-driven).\n   * **Publicity:** P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) using the issued ARC code prior to social media collection calls.\n\n2. **Freelancing & Part-Time Work Seminar (Term 2 / Post-Midterms):**\n   * **Classification:** Career-Related Seminar / Workshop.\n   * **DAAM Route:** General Pre-Activity (APS: 4–6 days) or SLIFE Career Activity via OCCS (5–8 days).\n   * **Requirements:** Full CPD Table 1 & Table 2 (with AET 5m before end), speaker outline (Pauline S. Galias), explicit \"Personal Lived Experience / No Platform Endorsement\" verbal and visual disclaimer, and room reservation ticket.\n\n---\n\n### Included Documents\n- `Freelancing_Seminar_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the live seminar.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every Pre-Act submission, collection tally, and P&M approval here to maintain a strict paper trail.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | Equipment Exchange Pre-Act (`Donation Drive`) | Pending | Lead time: 5–8 working days via SLIFE |\n| | Drop-Off Bin Lobby Reservation (SJ & Andrew) | Pending | 100% Free under RRS Sec 6.5/6.11 |\n| | P&M Submission (Donation Call) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n"}]},"project-8":{"name":"Project 8 Fundraising Local Business","files":[{"filename":"Local_Business_Fair_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Local Business Support Fair (Fundraiser)\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Local Business Support Fair\n- **Nature of Activity:** Fundraising & Partnership\n- **Type of Activity:** Selling / Bazaar\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 15 / 500\n- **Date:** February 25 - 27, 2027\n- **Time:** 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM\n- **Venue:** Marian Quadrangle (or equivalent open grounds)\n- **Project Head(s):** Director for External and Internal Linkages\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* The Student Emergency Fund and the Research Grants Pool require sustainable, reliable funding to remain operational. Relying solely on internal organizational funds limits the scope of aid we can provide.\n*(Part 2)* By partnering with local businesses around the campus, we can host a centralized fundraising fair. This provides valuable marketing and sales for our local partners while generating direct proceeds for our student welfare programs.\n*(Part 3)* This fair will act as the primary capital generator for the term, directly converting community engagement into financial support for science students in need.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To generate capital to fund the Student Emergency Fund and the Research Grants Pool.\n2. To provide a high-visibility marketing and sales platform for 3 to 5 local business partners.\n3. To foster stronger ties between the College of Science student body and the local campus business community.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM DESIGN**\n*(Applicable for each day of the 3-day fair)*\n- 8:00 AM - 9:00 AM: Ingress and Booth Setup\n- 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM: Fair Operations (Selling, Promotions, Student Engagement)\n- 5:00 PM - 6:00 PM: Egress and Daily Clean-up\n- 6:00 PM - 7:00 PM: Daily Remittance and Accounting of Proceeds\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Physical Booth Rentals (Tents/Tables): Php 3,000.00\n- **Total:** Php 3,000.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Organizational Funds: Php 3,000.00\n- **Total:** Php 3,000.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n- Projected Booth Fees / Percentage of Sales from 5 Partners: Php 10,000.00\n- **Total Projected Income:** Php 10,000.00\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Accumulated Operational Funds: N/A\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 3,000.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 3,000.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. PROVISIONS FOR PROFIT AND LOSS**\nAll net proceeds (Projected: Php 7,000.00) will be strictly divided: 70% to the Student Emergency Fund and 30% to the Research Grants Pool. The Chief Operating Officer and Chairperson for Finance shall be held liable and will shoulder any remaining balance or losses incurred by the project.\n"},{"filename":"README.md","content":"# Pre-Acts & Clearances: Fundraising & Local Business Collaboration\n**Project 8 — Science College Government (SCG) AY 2026–2027**\n\n---\n\n### General Status & DAAM Classification\nProject 8 generates sustainable community revenue to fund student emergency micro-grants and research awards, while actively guiding students to affordable food options around campus:\n\n1. **Taft Food Crawl: COS Student Food & Local Business Guide (Nov 4, 11, 18, 25, 2026 — GOSM Row 86):**\n   * **Classification:** Collaborative Media Coverage & Local Business Support Guide.\n   * **DAAM Route:** **`Media Coverage` (processed through APS)** with `Others: Digital Video Series & Food Map Infographic`.\n   * **Lead Time:** **4–6 working days**.\n   * **Collaboration:** Joint initiative with **FOCUS2026 (Batch 126 Government)**.\n   * **Budget:** ₱0.00 council funds (student-filmed vlogs, smartphone production, digital distribution).\n   * **Required Documentation:**\n     1. A-Form submitted to APS with joint signatures from SCG President and FOCUS2026 President.\n     2. CPD Table 1 detailing video production concepts, student affordability tiers, and map distribution channels.\n     3. N/A Breakdown of Expenses Table (₱0.00).\n   * **Publicity:** P&M clearance (24-hour lead time) for every individual vlog reel and infographic release under the approved ARC code.\n\n2. **Local Business Support Fair (Term 2 / Post-Midterms):**\n   * **Classification:** Commercial Fundraising Collaboration & Community Fair.\n   * **DAAM Route:** Special Approval Slip (**SCT: 1–3 working days**) and General Pre-Activity (**APS: 4–6 working days**).\n   * **Partner Agreements:** Strict **External Memorandum of Agreement (External MOA: 14-day minimum lead time via MST)** required per participating merchant.\n   * **Financial Accountability:** All proceeds remitted directly into SCG Depository Fund (DF), split 70% to Emergency Micro-Grants and 30% to Research Grants, followed by an FRA Report filed within 1 week of FAO confirmation.\n\n---\n\n### Included Documents\n- `Local_Business_Fair_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the fundraiser event.\n- `Taft_Food_Crawl_PPR.md` — Project Proposal Report for the joint Taft Food Crawl & Local Business Guide.\n\n---\n\n### Changelog\n*Log every Pre-Act submission, MOA status, and P&M approval here to maintain a strict paper trail.*\n\n| Date | Item / Action | Status | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| | Taft Food Crawl Pre-Act (`Media Coverage`) | Pending | Joint filing with FOCUS2026 (Lead time: 4–6 days) |\n| | P&M Submission (Food Crawl Vlogs & Map) | Pending | Lead time: 24 hours (requires approved ARC) |\n| | External MOA Submissions (Local Biz Fair) | Pending | Lead time: 14 working days minimum via MST |\n"},{"filename":"Taft_Food_Crawl_PPR.md","content":"# PROJECT PROPOSAL (PPR)\n## Taft Food Crawl: COS Student Food & Local Business Guide (with Batch 126)\n\n**I. ACTIVITY DETAILS**\n- **Title of Activity:** Taft Food Crawl: COS Student Food & Local Business Guide (with Batch 126)\n- **Nature of Activity:** Student Service\n- **Type of Activity:** Media Coverage (Digital Video Series & Food Map Infographic)\n- **ENMP/ENP:** 12 / 1,500\n- **Target Dates:** November 4, 11, 18, 25, 2026 (Weekly Episodic Drop)\n- **Venue / Medium:** SCG Official Facebook/Instagram Pages, TikTok/Reels, H2Zero.ai Platform\n- **Project Head(s):** Pauline S. Galias (President), Director for External and Internal Linkages, Director for Creatives, Batch 126 Representatives (FOCUS2026)\n- **Collaborating Units:** FOCUS2026 (Batch 126 Government)\n\n**II. BRIEF CONTEXT/PERSPECTIVE OF THE ACTIVITY**\n*(Part 1)* Science undergraduate students—especially newly matriculated frosh (Batch 126) and financial aid scholars—frequently face dining budget anxiety and unfamiliarity with affordable food options surrounding the DLSU Manila campus. High commercial dining prices often make socializing over meals stressful for students on limited allowances.\n*(Part 2)* In partnership with Batch 126 representatives, the Science College Government (SCG) will curate an organic, student-centered culinary survival guide. Through four engaging, vlog-style video reels and skits, student hosts explore popular and hidden food spots across multiple categories (chicken, Vietnamese/Asian bowls, budget carinderias, coffee/study havens, and desserts), spotlighting high-nutrition meals priced between ₱50 and ₱100.\n*(Part 3)* Alongside the episodic video series, a comprehensive, high-resolution Taft Food Map Infographic will be published and pinned across SCG platforms. This initiative supports local campus-adjacent micro-enterprises while ensuring science scholars feel empowered and included in campus social life.\n\n**III. OBJECTIVES**\n1. To catalog and map at least 40 campus-adjacent food establishments across 5 distinct cuisine and utility categories.\n2. To highlight affordable ₱50–₱100 meal alternatives for science scholars to alleviate dining budget anxiety and foster social inclusion.\n3. To collaborate with Batch 126 student representatives in scripting, filming, and publishing 4 engaging food review vlogs to welcome frosh students.\n4. To establish organic community goodwill with local food merchants prior to future institutional fundraiser partnerships.\n\n**IV. COMPREHENSIVE PROGRAM & ROLLOUT DESIGN**\n- **Episode 1 (November 4, 2026):** *The Taft Chicken Run* — Comparing fast-casual chicken spots, portion sizes, and budget hacks (24 Chicken, Bok, Uncle John's).\n- **Episode 2 (November 11, 2026):** *Vietnamese & Asian Comfort Bowls* — Exploring affordable pho, banh mi, and noodles for rainy study days.\n- **Episode 3 (November 18, 2026):** *The Scholar Survival Guide: ₱50–₱100 Carinderia Secrets* — Spotlighting Agno Food Court, Bloemen Hall, Castro St., and Leon Guinto carinderias.\n- **Episode 4 & Map Release (November 25, 2026):** *Late-Night Coffee & Study Havens* — Reviewing study spots with outlets and Wi-Fi, accompanied by the public release of the downloadable Taft Food Map Infographic on H2Zero.ai.\n\n**V. BREAKDOWN OF EXPENSES**\n- Production Equipment (Student Smartphones / Personal Stabilizers): Php 0.00\n- Digital Publishing & Distribution: Php 0.00\n- **Total Projected Expenses:** Php 0.00\n\n**VI. ALLOCATION OF EXPENSES**\n- Council Operational Funds: Php 0.00\n- **Total:** Php 0.00\n\n**VII. PROJECTED INCOME**\n- Commercial Sponsorships / Ads: Php 0.00 (Strict Non-Endorsement Policy)\n- **Total Projected Income:** Php 0.00\n\n**VIII. SUMMARY OF FUNDS**\n- Total Cash for Disbursement: Php 0.00\n- Less: Total Projected Expenses: Php 0.00\n- **REMAINING BALANCE:** Php 0.00\n\n**IX. GOVERNANCE & NON-ENDORSEMENT PROVISIONS**\nAll featured food establishments are selected through organic student recommendations and strict hygiene/safety standards. No paid sponsorships, advertising fees, or commercial gratuities are accepted. All creative materials adhere to USG P&M guidelines with required 24-hour clearance prior to publication.\n"}]}},"globalDocs":[{"filename":"00_Comms_Protocol.md","name":"Comms Protocol","content":"# Internal Communications Protocol\n\n## 1. Reporting Cadences\n- **Director-to-Chief (Weekly):** Every Friday by 5:00 PM, Directors must submit a bulleted status update on their assigned projects to their respective Chief (Chief of Staff, Operations, or Communications). Updates must explicitly highlight blocked tasks.\n- **Chief-to-President (Biweekly):** Chiefs will consolidate reports and meet with the President biweekly to assess overall platform health, DAAM approval statuses, and budget burn rates.\n\n## 2. Escalation Path\nIf a Director is stuck, a dependency is blocked, or a hard deadline (e.g., DAAM 14-day rule) is at risk, follow this exact escalation path:\n\n1. **24 Hours Blocked:** Director notifies their respective Chief via the internal messaging channel. The Chief attempts to unblock the issue directly.\n2. **48 Hours Blocked:** The Chief escalates the issue to the President.\n3. **72 Hours Blocked / Imminent Blackout Risk:** The President calls an emergency alignment meeting with the affected Director and Chief to trigger the fallback plan (e.g., moving an event post-midterms, triggering Plan B funding).\n4. **Administrative Blockade:** If the blockage is due to administrative non-cooperation (e.g., Dean refuses LOA, DAAM sits on a Pre-Act), the President and Chief of Staff will assume direct control of communications with the admin office to force a resolution.\n"},{"filename":"00_Handover_Notes.md","name":"Handover Notes","content":"# Handover Notes\n\nIf a responsible Director is suddenly unavailable due to academic workload, health, or turnover, this document outlines who assumes immediate operational control of their project and where all necessary files are located.\n\n### Project 1: Student Resource & Academic Support Hub\n- **Primary:** Director for Academics / Director for R&D\n- **Fallback:** Chief of Staff\n- **File Location:** `/Project_1_Student_Resource_Hub/`\n- **Key Note:** Ensure the OSFA verification dates and Dean's LOA are maintained. Do not let the Scholarship Calculator fall out of date.\n\n### Project 2: Semana ng Siyensya\n- **Primary:** Director for Academics / Chief of Operations\n- **Fallback:** President\n- **File Location:** `/Project_2_Semana_ng_Siyensya/`\n- **Key Note:** Ensure venue bookings and college approvals are secured at least 30 days prior.\n\n### Project 3: Transparency & Accountability Platform\n- **Primary:** Director for Student Services / Director for Finance\n- **Fallback:** Chief of Staff\n- **File Location:** `/Project_3_Transparency_Platform/`\n- **Key Note:** The 72-hour ledger update mandate cannot pause. If Dir. Finance is out, the Chief of Staff temporarily logs expenditures.\n\n### Project 4: Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation Initiative\n- **Primary:** Director for Advocacy\n- **Fallback:** Chief of Staff\n- **File Location:** `/Project_4_Inclusion_Accessibility/`\n- **Key Note:** Ensure the 90-day response deadline on the Lab Audit is still being actively tracked against the Dean's Office.\n\n### Project 5: Queer & Trans Identity Education Series\n- **Primary:** Director for Advocacy\n- **Fallback:** Director for National Affairs\n- **File Location:** `/Project_5_Queer_Trans_Education/`\n- **Key Note:** Maintain strict adherence to the medically-reviewed disclaimers on the Shanghay Laya website. Never skip the vetting of speaker credentials.\n\n### Project 6: Student Emergency Response & Relief Program\n- **Primary:** Director for Student Services\n- **Fallback:** Director for Logistics / Chief of Operations\n- **File Location:** `/Project_6_Emergency_Response/`\n- **Key Note:** The COS Pantry cannot close. Chief of Operations assumes physical restocking duties and usage logging immediately.\n\n### Project 7: Student Financial Empowerment Initiative\n- **Primary:** Director for Student Services\n- **Fallback:** Director for EXT/INT Linkages\n- **File Location:** `/Project_7_Financial_Empowerment/`\n- **Key Note:** Ensure the Freelancing Seminar archive is safely uploaded to the Hub within 48 hours.\n\n### Project 8: Fundraising & Local Business Collaboration Initiative\n- **Primary:** Director for EXT/INT Linkages\n- **Fallback:** Chief Communications\n- **File Location:** `/Project_8_Fundraising_Local_Business/`\n- **Key Note:** If a partner drops out, the 70/30 split logic remains identical. Chief Comms triggers the Plan B Crowdfunding Drive if the 0-partner fallback is reached.\n"},{"filename":"00_Master_Execution_Tracker.md","name":"Master Execution Tracker","content":"# Master Execution Tracker (AY 2026–2027 — Term 1)\r\n\r\n## 1. Project Status Overview (12 Final GOSM Initiatives)\r\n| Initiative | Owning Director(s) | Category & Type | Date / Timeline | Budget |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 1. H2Zero.ai (Offline Study Companion) | Dir. R&D | Early Processed / Standalone App | Oct 7, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 2. SCG Centralized Student Portal | Dir. Academics & Dir. Student Services | Yearlong / Integrated Web Portal | Yearlong | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 3. Women and Minorities in STEM | Dir. Promotions & Dir. Advocacy | Yearlong / Media Coverage & Archive | Yearlong | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 4. HomeCOStasis | Dir. Student Services | Termlong / Student Guide | Oct 14, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 5. COS Bulletin Board | Dir. Student Services | Termlong / Telegram Hub | Oct 14, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 6. Sakuna Relief (Food Security Pantry) | Dir. Student Welfare | Termlong / Emergency Assistance | Oct 7, 2026 | ₱3,000.00 (OF) |\r\n| 7. Equipment & Apparel Exchange | Dir. Student Services | Termlong / Donation Drive | Oct 14, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 8. Taft Food Crawl (Local Biz Guide) | Dir. Promotions & Dir. EXT/INT | Multiple Dates / Video Series & Guide | Oct 19, 20, 21, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 9. Scientific Spelling Bee: Biology Ed. | Dir. Academics & Dir. Promotions | Single Date / Competition | Oct 28, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 10. SCG General Assembly & Training | Secretary & Dir. Logistics | Single Date / Internal Assembly | Oct 21, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 11. LaTeX Essentials Seminar | Dir. Academics | Single Date / Academic Training | Nov 4, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n| 12. Shanghay Laya Platform Launch | Dir. Advocacy & Dir. Promotions | Single Date / Platform Launch | Nov 7, 2026 | ₱0.00 |\r\n\r\n## 2. Executive & Chief Verification Responsibilities\r\n- **Secretary (to the President):** Executive right-hand and master timeline custodian. Maintains the Master Tracker across all 12 initiatives, tracks deliverables across all three Chiefs, coordinates calendar bookings, and delivers daily briefings and critical alerts to President Pauline.\r\n- **Chief of Staff:** Oversees Academics, R&D, Student Services, and Student Welfare. Enforces policy compliance, administrative letters (Dean's LOA), and monitors student grievance protocols.\r\n- **Chief of Operations:** Oversees Logistics, Finance, and Documentations. Verifies physical event logistics (venues, floor plans), DAAM Pre/Post-Act paperwork, and strict USG financial compliance (SCTs, FRA reports).\r\n- **Chief of Communications:** Oversees Creatives, Promotions, EXT/INT Linkages, and Advocacy. Enforces brand identity, 24-hour P&M clearance pipelines, video production schedules, and external sponsorships.\r\n\r\n## 3. General Assembly Talking Points (Pauline S. Galias)\r\n- Rooting governance in permanent institutional infrastructure, not performative one-off events.\r\n- Shifting from vague promises to concrete digital tools: H2Zero.ai, Centralized Student Portal, and Academic Pathing.\r\n- Protecting student welfare through confidential grievance intake (with formal elected-officer escalation) and real emergency food assistance (Sakuna Relief Pantry).\r\n- Championing inclusive science through visible representation: Women & Minorities in STEM documentaries, LaTeX seminar, and Shanghay Laya.\r\n- Empowering local student culture through the Taft Food Crawl and student discount partnerships.\r\n\r\n## 4. Officer Onboarding Snapshots\r\n- **Secretary (to the President):** You are the administrative anchor and master tracker. You ensure no deadline is missed, maintain the master council calendar, and keep the President updated on all deliverable milestones.\r\n- **Director for Academics:** You lead educational empowerment — LaTeX Essentials Seminar, Biology Spelling Bee, and the prerequisite guides on the Portal.\r\n- **Director for R&D:** You treat undergraduate research as a professional pursuit. You manage the Grants Portal, Research Job Board, Readiness Survey, and H2Zero.ai integration.\r\n- **Director for Student Services:** You drive daily campus relief — HomeCOStasis lounge, Equipment & Apparel Exchange, and the real-time COS Bulletin Board.\r\n- **Director for Student Welfare:** You are the confidential frontline for student casework (intake and referral to the President) and lead the operational logistics of the Sakuna Emergency Relief Pantry.\r\n- **Director for Documentations:** You maintain the paper trail. You track every Pre-Act, APS, SLIFE, and Post-Act in the DAAM pipeline so the council remains 100% compliant.\r\n- **Director for Logistics:** You secure all physical spaces (E-Classroom, Yuchengco, Andrew, Amphitheater), transport supplies, and run on-ground staging.\r\n- **Director for Finance:** You oversee the money movement. You handle the sole ₱3,000 OF for the Pantry, manage transparency logs, and produce complete FRA liquidations.\r\n- **Director for Creatives:** You build the visual identity and digital interfaces. You guarantee all pubmats meet design standards and clear P&M with 24 hours lead time.\r\n- **Director for Promotions:** You are our multimedia production engine. You direct scriptwriters, actors, and videographers for reels and documentaries, and design immersive booth activations.\r\n- **Director for EXT/INT Linkages:** You build external partnerships, negotiate merchant MOAs for the Taft Food Crawl, and secure sponsor support.\r\n- **Director for Advocacy:** You ground diversity and rights in institutional action, leading the Shanghay Laya launch and minority representation archives.\r\n"},{"filename":"00_PreAct_Classification.md","name":"PreAct Classification","content":"# Pre-Activity (Pre-Act) Classification & DAAM Compliance Framework\n\n**Science College Government (SCG) — De La Salle University — Manila**  \n**Academic Year 2026–2027**  \n**President:** Pauline S. Galias  \n**Core Policy:** *\"Para sa Agham, na Ramdam ng Taumbayan\"*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Mandate: Universal Pre-Activity Requirement\n\nUnder the **DLSU USG Department of Activity Approval and Monitoring (DAAM) Operations Manual (AY 2025–2026)**, **EVERY SINGLE INITIATIVE in the approved GOSM requires a formally filed and approved Pre-Activity Form (Pre-Act) prior to any public execution, rollout, or announcement.** \n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **The Umbrella Website Strategy (Administrative Relief & Defense Strength):**  \n> Rather than drowning the Department of Documentations in 7 separate, redundant Pre-Acts for individual web pages, all purely digital student services and transparency tools are consolidated under **ONE Master Pre-Act** for the **SCG Centralized Student Portal** (`thru APS; Others`).\n> \n> * **1 Master Pre-Act = 1 Master Activity Reference Code (ARC)**\n> * Once approved, all subsequent pubmats, feature rollouts, and tool explainers are cleared via **Publicity Materials (P&M) with a 24-hour lead time** using that master ARC!\n> * This simultaneously protects the council at the GOSM Defense from accusations of \"GOSM padding\" or splitting one website into multiple rows.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DAAM Processing Categories & Standard Workflows\n\n### Category A: The Centralized Master Web Infrastructure\nOperates as the official modular student portal hosted client-side at zero cost.\n\n*   **DAAM Processing Route:** General Pre-Activity (**`thru APS; Others`**)\n*   **Lead Time:** **4–6 working days** prior to public rollout\n*   **Required Documentation:**\n    1.  **Activity Form (A-Form):** Marked *\"Online Activity: YES\"*, *\"Activity in GOSM: YES\"*, *\"Activity Type: thru APS; Others\"*, with description `Others: Centralized Digital Student Portal & Modular Governance Platform`.\n    2.  **Comprehensive Project Description (CPD):** **Table 1 Only** (per DAAM Manual Page 119 for non-synchronous online activities). Table 1 itemizes all 7 functional modules:\n        *   *Module 1:* Scholarship Portal & Grade Calculator\n        *   *Module 2:* Academic Pathing Tool (Prerequisite Flowcharts)\n        *   *Module 3:* Centralized Grievance Channel (with External Mediator)\n        *   *Module 4:* Financial Transparency Ledger & Suggestion Tab (72-hour rule)\n        *   *Module 5:* Student Rights Charter (Digital Handbook)\n        *   *Module 6:* Syllabus Transparency Repository (Dean's LOA advance study)\n        *   *Module 7:* Free Software Directory (FLOSS open-source tools)\n    3.  **Breakdown of Expenses Table:** Retained in full with **\"N/A\"** declared (₱0.00 council funds; zero commercial server costs).\n*   **Ongoing Publicity (P&M):** All module explainers, infographics, and rights reels use this single approved ARC with a 24-hour turnaround.\n\n---\n\n### Category B: Standalone Digital & Media Initiatives\nInitiatives with distinct technical architectures, filming requirements, or prior administration continuity:\n\n1.  **H2Zero.ai (Row 5 - Early Processed):** Standalone offline desktop AI study companion (Tauri/Rust architecture, zero cloud server calls, zero water/energy footprint). Filed as General Pre-Activity (`thru APS; Others`).\n2.  **Women & Minorities in STEM (Row 33 - Yearlong):** Documentary-style video featurettes, interviews, and permanent research archives. Filed under `Media Coverage` with strict RA 10173 signed consent forms.\n3.  **HomeCOStasis (Row 59 - Termlong):** Centralized Google Drive/Sheets navigation guide refreshed for Batch 126 frosh and shiftees. Continued under prior ARC (`G-SCG-25260011`).\n4.  **COS Bulletin Board (Row 60 - Termlong):** Real-time Telegram broadcast and announcement hub. Continued under prior ARC (`G-SCG-25260016`).\n5.  **Shanghay Laya (Row 116 - Single Date):** Public launch of the queer/trans health and educational repository, filed under `thru APS; Others` with a signed Legal Disclaimer Review.\n\n---\n\n### Category C: Physical Events, Drives & Collaborative Series\nInitiatives involving physical campus venues, physical donations, direct funding, or inter-unit collaboration:\n\n1.  **Sakuna Relief: Food Security Pantry (Row 61):** Filed via SLIFE (`thru SLIFE; Others`, 5–8 days) with ₱3,000.00 OF budget, PRS memo, and St. Joseph Hall 1st Floor Front Lobby exhibit ticket (RRS Sec 6.5). Post-act ADM liquidation due within 5 days.\n2.  **Equipment & Apparel Exchange (Row 62):** Physical donation drive for lab gowns and calculators, filed via SLIFE (`Donation Drive`, 5–8 days) with collection bins at SJ and Andrew Hall.\n3.  **Taft Food Crawl with Batch 126 (Row 86):** Joint media coverage series with FOCUS2026, filed under `Media Coverage` (4–6 days).\n4.  **Scientific Terminologies Spelling Bee: Biology Edition (Row 113):** Academic contest in Teresa Yuchengco Hall Y 508 Seminar Room (100% Free for $\\le 2$ hrs per RRS Sec 8.5.2). Filed via APS (`Contest`, 4–6 days).\n5.  **SCG GA & Officer Training (Row 114):** Operational training and assembly in Teresa Yuchengco Hall Y 508. Filed via APS (`General Assembly`, 4–6 days).\n6.  **LaTeX Essentials Seminar (Row 115):** Hands-on typeset masterclass in Br. Andrew Gonzalez Hall A 903 Lecture Room (100% Free for $\\le 2$ hrs per RRS Sec 8.5.2). Filed via APS (`Seminar/Workshop`, 4–6 days).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Master Term 1 Pre-Act & GOSM Alignment Matrix (All 12 Initiatives)\n\n| Row | Initiative Name | GOSM Col I (`ACTIVITY TYPE`) | GOSM Col J (`OTHERS:`) | DAAM Processing Route | Lead Time | Key Required Attachments |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **5** | **H2Zero.ai: Standalone AI Desktop App** | `thru APS; Others` | `Others: Standalone Offline AI Desktop Application` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1, N/A Expense Table, Early Processing Letter (EPL) |\n| **32** | **SCG Centralized Student Portal** | `thru APS; Others` | `Others: Centralized Digital Student Portal & Modular Governance Platform` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 (7 modules itemized), N/A Expense Table, Mediator sign-off |\n| **33** | **Women & Minorities in STEM** | `Media Coverage` | `Others: Video Documentary Featurettes & Digital Research Archive` | Media Coverage (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 & Video Concept Plan, RA 10173 Opt-In Consent Form templates |\n| **59** | **HomeCOStasis Student Guide** | `thru APS; Others` | `Others: Centralized Digital Student Navigation Guide` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1, N/A Expense Table, ARC Continuity ref (`G-SCG-25260011`) |\n| **60** | **COS Bulletin Board** | `thru APS; Others` | `Others: Real-Time Telegram Broadcast & Announcement Channel` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1, N/A Expense Table, ARC Continuity ref (`G-SCG-25260016`) |\n| **61** | **Sakuna Relief: Food Security Pantry** | `thru SLIFE; Others` | `Others: Emergency Disaster Relief & Welfare Pantry` | SLIFE Activity | 5–8 days | A-Form, LOE (₱3k OF), PRS, Lobby Reservation Ticket (St. Joseph Lobby, RRS Sec 6.5) |\n| **62** | **Equipment & Apparel Exchange** | `Donation Drive` | `Others:` | SLIFE Donation Drive | 5–8 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1, Collection Bin Lobby Ticket (SJ & Andrew), Condition Protocol |\n| **86** | **Taft Food Crawl (with Batch 126)** | `Media Coverage` | `Others: Digital Video Series & Food Map Infographic` | Media Coverage (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 & Video Concept Plan, Joint Endorsement with FOCUS2026 |\n| **113** | **Spelling Bee: Biology Edition** | `Contest` | `Others:` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 & 2 (AET 5m before end), Room Confirmation (Yuchengco Y 508) |\n| **114** | **SCG GA & Officer Training** | `General Assembly/Recognition` | `Others:` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 & 2 (AET 5m before end), Room Confirmation (Yuchengco Y 508) |\n| **115** | **LaTeX Essentials Seminar** | `Seminar/Workshop` | `Others:` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1 & 2 (AET 5m before end), Room Confirmation (Andrew A 903) |\n| **116** | **Shanghay Laya (Platform Launch)** | `thru APS; Others` | `Others: Digital Educational Platform Launch` | General Pre-Activity (APS) | 4–6 days | A-Form, CPD Table 1, N/A Expense Table, Legal Disclaimer Sign-Off Sheet |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key Directives for Council Officers\n\n1.  **Director for Documentations:** File the **SCG Centralized Student Portal Pre-Act** at least **2 weeks prior to Day 1 of Term 1**. This single filing unlocks the master ARC code for all 7 web modules.\n2.  **Director for Creatives:** For all social media pubmats, reels, and explainers covering the calculator, syllabus portal, software directory, and rights charter, submit P&M clearance under the **Master Student Portal ARC code** with a 24-hour turnaround.\n3.  **Director for Logistics:** Ensure all room reservations for Oct 21, Oct 28, and Nov 11 are strictly scheduled for $\\le 2.0$ hours under the **100% Free Medium Venue provision (RRS Sec 8.5.2)**.\n4.  **Director for Finance:** The sole Operational Fund requisition across the entire council is **₱3,000.00 for Sakuna Relief Pantry (`T1 LOE` Row 13)**. All other 11 initiatives are strictly **₱0.00**.\n"},{"filename":"00_Project_Defense_Briefing.md","name":"Project Defense Briefing","content":"# Project Defense Briefing\r\n\r\n## P1: Student Resource Hub\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** A centralized digital infrastructure that removes systemic barriers to academic progression and research funding.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** How will you keep this updated when the curriculum and scholarships constantly change?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Sustainability):** *Answer:* We built this on open-source, easily transferable static sites, eliminating long-term maintenance costs and making turnovers seamless.\r\n\r\n## P2: Semana ng Siyensya (COS Week)\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** A week-long academic and gaming celebration that directly funds the Food Security Pantry and Emergency Micro-Grants through merchandise drives.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** How will you ensure the academic competitions don't interfere with students' classes?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Incentivization):** *Answer:* We are currently coordinating with faculty to provide academic incentives (e.g., extra credit) to encourage participation without adding burden.\r\n\r\n## P3: Transparency Platform\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** A platform holding the council to the same rigorous accountability standards it demands from the administration.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** How do you guarantee the council won't just cover up grievances filed against its own officers?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Legal Exposure):** *Answer:* We employ strict moderation guidelines and a 2-officer approval requirement before hiding any personal attacks on the Suggestion Tab, protecting students and officers alike.\r\n\r\n## P4: Inclusion, Accessibility & Representation\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** Moving beyond performative awareness by demanding structural, physical, and historical accessibility in the College of Science.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** What happens if the administration completely ignores your Laboratory Accessibility Audit?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (FSL Costs):** *Answer:* Solved via a formal MOA with a Deaf community organization, ensuring professional, sustained instruction rather than relying on one-off volunteers.\r\n\r\n## P5: Queer & Trans Education Series\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** Rooting queer and trans identities in rigorous biological, sociological, and historical research to counter unscientific prejudice.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** What if the Shanghay Laya website provides dangerous medical advice?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Admin Pushback):** *Answer:* Defended via the Student Rights Charter and our strict reliance on credentialed, peer-reviewed academics.\r\n\r\n## P6: Student Emergency Response\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** A rapid-response crisis ecosystem that aggressively cuts through bureaucracy to deliver aid when students need it most.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** Why isn't the pantry logging names to prevent hoarding?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Data Privacy):** *Answer:* The Welfare Registry complies strictly with RA 10173; it is opt-in only, and we enforce a mandatory termly data wipe.\r\n\r\n## P7: Financial Empowerment Initiative\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** Directly mitigating the hidden costs of a science degree through resource sharing and freelancing upskilling.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** Is the Student Council endorsing the freelancing platforms mentioned in the seminar?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Quality Control):** *Answer:* The Equipment Exchange mandates basic condition checks and features a clear \"No Implied Warranty\" disclaimer.\r\n\r\n## P8: Fundraising & Local Business\r\n- **Elevator Pitch:** Transforming mutual community support into sustainable, reliable financial aid for the Emergency Fund and Research Grants Pool.\r\n- **Hardest FAQ:** What if we don't get any partners and the fair gets cancelled? Does the Emergency Fund run out?\r\n- **Panel Follow-Up (Proceeds Transparency):** *Answer:* We enforce a strict 70/30 split tracked visibly on the Project 3 public Ledger.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Contingency Question\r\n**Q: \"If you had to cut one project due to budget or time constraints, which one and why?\"**\r\n**A:** \"We would reluctantly cut the *Pre-Colonial Gender & Trans History Session (Project 5)*. While highly valuable, our absolute, non-negotiable priority is immediate student survival and welfare. We will never compromise the Emergency Response Program (P6), the Transparency Platform (P3), or the Resource Hub (P1). If necessary, the history session's insights can be integrated into the Science Forum so we can preserve resources for direct aid.\"\r\n"},{"filename":"00_Risk_Register.md","name":"Risk Register","content":"# Risk Register\r\n\r\n| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **P1: Scholarship Criteria Changes** | High | High | ADSO point of contact established; criteria verified termly. | Dir. Academics |\r\n| **P1: Syllabus LOA Rejected** | Medium | High | Secure physical/digital signature from Dean early before launch. | Dir. Academics |\r\n| **P2: Low Event Turnout** | Medium | Medium | Heavy promotions, coordinate for faculty incentives (extra credit). | Dir. Academics |\r\n| **P2: Merch Sales Miss Targets** | Medium | High | Pre-order system to avoid overstocking and guarantee profit margins. | Dir. Finance |\r\n| **P3: Admin Ignores Grievance** | High | Medium | Only promise visibility. Document unresolved cases publicly (with consent). | Dir. Stud. Services |\r\n| **P3: Suggestion Tab Toxicity** | High | Low | 2-officer approval required to moderate personal attacks; policy criticisms stay. | Dir. Stud. Services |\r\n| **P4: FSL MOA Fails** | Low | High | External MOA negotiated early. Will not rely on single volunteer. | Dir. EXT/INT |\r\n| **P4: Lab Audit Access Denied** | Medium | Medium | 90-day deadline established. Publish audit publicly if ignored. | Dir. Advocacy |\r\n| **P4: STEM Archive Privacy Breach**| Low | High | Strict RA 10173 opt-in consent form mandated prior to posting. | Dir. Advocacy |\r\n| **P5: Unqualified Speaker** | Low | High | Strict vetting: only academic/peer-reviewed researchers invited. | Dir. Advocacy |\r\n| **P5: Legal Liability (Hormones)** | Medium | High | Legal disclaimer review dependency; link only to licensed endocrinologists. | Dir. Advocacy |\r\n| **P6: Calamity Trigger Ambiguity** | Low | Medium | Strict reliance on PAGASA / LGU declarations for Tier 3/4. | Dir. Finance |\r\n| **P6: Welfare Registry Privacy Leak**| Low | Critical | RA 10173 compliance. Data wiped termly unless re-consented. | Dir. Stud. Services |\r\n| **P7: Faulty Donated Equipment** | Medium | Low | Mandatory basic condition check; explicit \"No Implied Warranty\" disclaimer. | Dir. Stud. Services |\r\n| **P7: Misconstrued Endorsement** | Low | High | Verbal and visual disclaimers stating \"Lived Experience, No Endorsement.\" | President |\r\n| **P8: Partner Backs Out Mid-Term** | Medium | High | Rely on minimum 3-5 partners to dilute risk. | Dir. EXT/INT |\r\n| **P8: Zero Partners Secured (Plan B)**| Low | High | Publicly acknowledge shortfall; launch Emergency Crowdfunding Drive. | Dir. EXT/INT |\r\n| **General: Pre-Act Rejected Near Blackout**| Medium | High | Buffer lead times factored in. If rejected, move to Post-Midterms or Post-ILW. | Dir. Documentations |\r\n| **General: Officer Turnover Mid-Term**| Low | High | Strict Handover Notes and centralized execution plans ensure continuity. | Chief of Staff |\r\n"}]};